window.harnessChunks["rows-84246911231a89b2.js"]=["<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-20T21:45:19+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Technion-Kishony-lab/data-to-paper\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">data-to-paper</a><small>2023</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-20\">2026-09-20</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Technion-Kishony-lab/data-to-paper/tree/81df14c4b9600466e645c3b2b336cc54daa3df3a\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 81df14c4b9600466e645c3b2b336cc54daa3df3a\">81df14c &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously perform the environment-facing scientific work needed to transform project data and research state into executable analyses and research artifacts. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous datasets, generated-code errors, unexpected outputs/files, statistical-analysis requirements, intermediate product state and manuscript construction requirements. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose and revise analysis/code/content actions in response to current project products, execution results and validation/reviewer feedback. - Decision owner: the active first-party performer/code-writing agents in the hypothesis-testing workflow. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: stage runner, CodeRunner, debugger conversers, code extract/check logic, output requirements, product store, data chaining and configured iteration bounds. - Closure path: current scientific products/data -&gt; agent proposes code/content action -&gt; first-party runner executes/materializes it -&gt; output/error/product state returns -&gt; agent revises or advances. - Boundary reachability: these paths are the shipped hypothesis-testing research workflow invoked by the installable application/CLI, not benchmark-only examples. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: runtime and guardrails execute/check code, but the context-sensitive analysis/content choice and revision remain model-agent decisions. - Evidence: README.md, stepsrunner.py, requestcode.py. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material first-party S2 coordination path is established at the reviewed recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the workflow contains multiple performer/reviewer roles and research stages, but no concrete interference, conflict or oscillation among distinct S1 units is identified and attenuated by a dedicated coordination relation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for S2. - Decision owner: none established for S2. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: typed stage transitions, product passing, paired conversations, deterministic sequencing and rewind/retry utilities. - Closure path: these mechanisms sequence work and transmit review feedback; they do not close a cross-S1 interference-regulation loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: interacting agents and state transfer are real, but the reviewed paths are production/review dependencies rather than S2-specific conflict attenuation. - Evidence: stepsrunner.py, dualconverser.py. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no autonomous whole-system inside-and-now control function is established at the reviewed recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: stage completion, retries, rewinds, project parameters and review rounds constrain current work, but no first-party autonomous actor is shown holding a whole-project current view plus broad authority over shared resources/commitments/priorities. - Decisive decision or feedback right: stage flow and retry bounds are primarily encoded in deterministic runner logic and project parameters; local reviewers control product acceptance rather than organization-wide current control. - Decision owner: none established for S3. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: DataStepRunner/HypothesisTestingStepsRunner, stage enums, product readiness, retry/rewind helpers, project parameters and UI actions. - Closure path: configured stage/retry conditions alter control flow mechanically; no discretionary whole-system S3 owner is evidenced. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model agents make local scientific/product decisions, while the broader workflow sequence is framework-defined. - Evidence: stepsrunner.py, README.md. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: provide a separate challenge channel that reviews scientific/code/writing products and returns concrete corrective feedback before those products are accepted. - Disturbance / variety regulated: plausible-but-wrong analysis outputs, code/result problems, weak hypotheses/plans, writing defects and other product claims that ordinary performer generation may miss. - Decisive decision or feedback right: independently approve a product or issue constructive concerns that force another performer revision cycle. - Decision owner: the separate reviewer LLM conversation in ReviewDialogDualConverserGPT and review-specific subclasses; in code paths, review agents inspect executed outputs/code products separately from the generating/debugging conversation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: separate ConversationManager, review termination phrase, max review rounds, product extraction/checks, code-review prompts and rewind utilities. - Closure path: performer produces valid candidate -&gt; reviewer receives the candidate/background -&gt; reviewer approves or supplies concerns -&gt; concerns are appended to the performer conversation with explicit correction request -&gt; performer rewrites/revises -&gt; review repeats. - Boundary reachability: the review dialog base class is reused by goal, planning and manuscript-writing review steps in the shipped hypothesis-testing workflow; code review is also embedded in the standard code-product path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the reviewer is a separate agent conversation and owns the semantic approve/challenge judgment; deterministic loop code only routes/enforces that judgment. - Claim being audited: that a current scientific product/code result is adequate/correct enough to become accepted workflow state. - Ordinary reporting path: performer response plus normal generated/executed product state. - Complementary access path: separate reviewer conversation receives the product and relevant background/output context; code review can inspect created output contents. - Independence boundary: reviewer has its own conversation manager/role and does not share the performer&#39;s assistant role; review feedback is generated independently and then returned. - Who acts on findings: the original performer/code-writing agent revises the product in the next cycle. - Evidence: dualconverser.py, requestcode.py, stepsrunner.py. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A(P) - Function: use external literature/novelty evidence to develop or revise future research direction and return the selected direction into the current experimental plan, with both autonomous and Copilot parent-governed modes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: existing literature may make a candidate goal non-novel, external findings may change relevant hypotheses, and a human scientist may judge the proposed future direction inadequate. - Decisive decision or feedback right: in Autopilot, research/novelty agents judge literature-derived novelty and generate/refine the goal/hypothesis; in Copilot, human review can replace/supplement reviewer feedback and require revised research products. - Decision owner: autonomous research/reviewer agents in base mode; human scientist in the supported Copilot parent mode for the reviewed future-direction decision. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: literature-search agents, NoveltyAssessmentReview, ReGoalReviewGPT, stage return to GOAL, human-review application interactions, product storage and plan handoff. - Closure path: external literature -&gt; novelty/goal review -&gt; autonomous or parent judgment -&gt; if inadequate, workflow returns to goal generation/refinement -&gt; resulting goal/hypothesis/testing plan feeds data analysis and later research stages. - Boundary reachability: literature/novelty/goal refinement is part of the shipped hypothesis-testing runner, and README plus HumanReviewType/review interaction code expose Copilot human feedback through the same first-party workflow. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the base mode closes without human direction through model-agent novelty/refinement decisions; the separately supported Copilot mode intentionally transfers the relevant review/judgment right to the human and returns that decision into workflow state. - External distinction: retrieved scientific literature and most-similar-paper evidence. - Future / prospective distinction: whether the current research goal/hypothesis is novel/appropriate enough to warrant future experimentation. - Adaptation option generated: revised research goal/hypothesis and downstream hypothesis-testing plan. - Path back into current capability / S3: failed novelty sets regoal and returns to ScientificStage.GOAL; accepted/revised goal then feeds planning, code analysis and manuscript stages. - Evidence: stepsrunner.py, dualconverser.py, README.md. - Basis: structural -\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party identity/ultimate-policy closure is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: project parameters, research goals, human feedback and methodological guardrails constrain work, but no identity-level dispute is routed to an ultimate authority and returned as governing organizational policy. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for S5. - Decision owner: none established for S5. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: startup/project parameters, user research goal, coding guardrails, reviewer prompts and human-review UI. - Closure path: these controls constrain local research behavior directly; no identity/ultimate-policy issue -&gt; authoritative decision -&gt; returned policy loop is evidenced. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: neither autonomous agents nor the Copilot human are shown resolving an identity-level policy issue at this recursion. - Evidence: README.md, stepsrunner.py. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/c9da28372ac35ff5d8bc9a74679a6987a5d451df/assessments/data-to-paper.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit c9da28372ac35ff5d8bc9a74679a6987a5d451df\">c9da283 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-21T19:11:00+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/zhayujie/CowAgent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CowAgent</a><small>2022</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-20\">2026-09-20</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/zhayujie/CowAgent/tree/7c55a61e1bccb82c99d97a4a436f598c079f7c9d\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 7c55a61e1bccb82c99d97a4a436f598c079f7c9d\">7c55a61 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously transform user/scheduled/delegated objectives into environment-facing tool/skill work and returned outcomes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous user objectives, model uncertainty, tool results/errors, files/shell/browser/service state, channel messages, and follow-up observations encountered during a trajectory. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next reasoning/tool/skill action, incorporate returned observations, delegate bounded work when useful, or stop with a final response. - Decision owner: the running CowAgent Agent/model actor inside the first-party AgentStreamExecutor loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ChatService, AgentBridge, provider adapters, prompt/workspace construction, tool registry, permissions, session persistence, scheduler integration and context controls. - Closure path: each tool result, delegated result or new model observation returns into the first-party executor context and can change the next autonomous action until completion. - Boundary reachability: the credited executor/bridge/tool path is the normal shipped Web/channel runtime and is instantiated directly by standard CowAgent operation at the pinned revision. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic code supplies transport, limits and execution, while the model actor owns the discretionary next-action choice on each loop iteration. - Evidence: pinned agent/chat/service.py and bridge/agentbridge.py; README.md corroborates the shipped planning/tool execution boundary. - Basis: structural and explicit. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: model/provider/tool availability, permissions and iteration limits constrain the Agent&#39;s operational discretion but do not replace its next-action judgment.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate a concrete interference between sibling operational Agents when concurrent delegations target the same persistent relay conversation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: concurrent handoffs to one source→target relay session would otherwise interleave turns in the target Agent&#39;s transcript and corrupt the shared conversational state used by later delegated work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: serialize admission to that target relay session so exactly one delegated turn occupies it at a time; timeout rather than permit an overlapping handoff when the lock cannot be acquired within policy. - Decision owner: constructor-owned at the assessed boundary. The first-party S2-specific serialization primitive is complete and operational, but the conflict policy itself is deterministic/developer-selected rather than autonomously chosen by an Agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: per-relay relaylock, stable relay-session derivation, delegation roster/ACL, depth/cycle checks, timeout and synchronous result return. - Closure path: a source Agent&#39;s delegation blocks on the relay lock; after the prior target turn finishes and releases the lock, the next delegated target run executes against a non-interleaved transcript and its result returns to the waiting source Agent, changing that source&#39;s subsequent S1 behavior. - Boundary reachability: agentdelegate is a shipped team tool attached to normal team turns through AgentBridge; no downstream coordinator or test-only actor is required for the serialization path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Agents autonomously decide to delegate and select the teammate/task, but once two handoffs conflict the coordination choice is a fixed first-party lock policy. CowAgent therefore exposes a real S2-specific construction path without an autonomous owner of the conflict-regulation decision. - Evidence: pinned agent/tools/agentdelegate/agentdelegate.py and team-session support in bridge/agentbridge.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: other team features such as speaker selection, shared transcripts and ordinary handoff are not separately credited as S2 merely because they connect Agents. - Distinct S1 units: independently configured live CowAgent Agents with separate profiles/workspaces and first-party execution loops participating in one team conversation. - Inter-S1 disturbance: overlapping delegated turns to the same target relay session can interleave in the target transcript. - Attenuating coordination relation: the first-party per-relay lock queues competing handoffs and allows only one target turn at a time. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: the coordinated target result is returned synchronously to the delegating source Agent&#39;s tool loop; timeout/failure likewise returns a result that changes the source Agent&#39;s next decision. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the lock exists specifically because concurrent sibling handoffs would interfere by interleaving one operational transcript, and it changes admission/timing to suppress that identified disturbance before results feed back to the source Agent.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Function: regulate the currently configured CowAgent installation as a whole by changing which operational Agents are available, which models/capabilities they use, and which live channel instances/teams route work to them. - Disturbance / variety regulated: current mismatch between installed operational capacity and the operator&#39;s desired roster, agent availability, model/capability allocation, shared/own knowledge mode, default ownership and live channel/team bindings. - Decisive decision or feedback right: create/update/archive/delete an Agent, make an Agent default, enable/disable it, alter its model/skills/knowledge allocation, or rebind a running channel/team to a different Agent roster. - Decision owner: the authenticated self-hosted operator is the legitimate parent actor in this supported mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AgentAdminService, roster revision checks, Web authentication, registry/router rebuilding, scheduler reconciliation, targeted live-runtime eviction and hot channel rebinding. - Closure path: the operator submits a first-party Web/API decision; CowAgent persists it, updates the live registry/router or channel binding, starts/stops the affected schedulers and evicts changed Agent instances so subsequent current work is executed under the returned control decision. - Boundary reachability: the authenticated Web console and its /api/agents administration paths are a standard shipped self-hosted operating surface documented as the unified configuration hub, not a development-only control plane. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: no standard first-party autonomous Agent is given installation-wide roster/model/capability/channel authority. The decisive current-control choice comes from the authenticated operator; runtime code only validates, persists and enforces it. - Evidence: pinned agent/admin.py, channel/web/api/agents.py, and bridge/agentbridge.py scheduler/runtime reconciliation. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: this claim is limited to installation-level current capability/resource control. Per-task steering/cancel and permission enforcement alone would not establish S3, and the assessment does not treat every settings edit as an S3 decision. - Whole-system current view: AgentAdminService.snapshot() exposes the installation&#39;s full current Agent roster, default Agent and channel instances; the same authenticated surface operates over that roster rather than one worker-local state. - Current-control decision scope: availability of S1 units, current model/capability allocation, default Agent, channel/team routing and scheduler participation for the running installation.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no material first-party complementary and sufficiently independent audit function was established at the declared installation boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: none established as an S3 witness; ordinary tool feedback, run/event recording and Self-Evolution review do not provide a materially independent route to operational reality for checking production claims. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no S3-specific independent audit judgment/feedback right is supplied. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: execution/tool events, conversation/run persistence, evolution records/backups, logs and operator inspection surfaces. - Closure path: no independent audit finding→corrective-current-control loop was established; recorded events and evolution changes serve their primary execution/adaptation purposes. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: there is no qualifying audit function whose decisive judgment could be assigned to an autonomous Agent. - Evidence: pinned chat/executor event path, conversation storage, Web/admin surfaces, and agent/evolution/ review path. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a downstream deployment could add an independent verifier or external monitor, but that composed system would be outside this frozen first-party boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: convert externally observed conversation patterns and capability failures into prospective adaptations of reusable future capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing user preferences and recurring work patterns, skill defects discovered in real use, reusable workflows not yet encoded as capabilities, and unfinished work whose recurrence or omission would reduce future fit. - Decisive decision or feedback right: after an idle conversation, decide autonomously whether there is a clear durable signal and whether to patch an existing skill, create a new reusable skill, consolidate knowledge/memory, or take no adaptation action. - Decision owner: the isolated first-party Self-Evolution review Agent/model actor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: idle trigger thresholds, restricted evolution toolset, guarded workspace paths, pre-change backup, snapshot diff/change detection, transactional rollback and evolution records. - Closure path: the review Agent senses conversation-derived external signals, selects and writes an adaptation, the executor commits only real guarded changes, and later normal Agent runs load/use the revised skill/knowledge/persona state; if no clear signal exists it explicitly leaves capability unchanged. - Boundary reachability: Self-Evolution is started by normal AgentBridge initialization, its configuration class defaults enabled, and the standard pinned config-template.json ships selfevolutionenabled: true; the decision actor is therefore reachable in the supported distribution without borrowing a dogfood or contributor agent. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: trigger timing, backups and write guards are deterministic support, but the model review Agent owns the discretionary judgment of whether a past external interaction predicts future reusable need and which permitted adaptation to make. - Evidence: pinned agent/evolution/config.py, trigger.py, executor.py, prompts.py, bridge/agentbridge.py, config-template.json and docs/memory/self-evolution.mdx. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: simple memory consolidation alone is not the credited S4 witness. The positive mapping relies on the separately evidenced prospective skill-adaptation path: detecting a workflow likely to be wanted again or a skill defect exposed in use, autonomously generating an adaptation, and returning that change into later capability. - External distinction: the review consumes user conversation and actual skill/task experience from the environment, including repeated preferences, failures and emergent workflows. - Future / prospective distinction: the evolution prompt explicitly asks whether a workflow is reusable and likely to be wanted again, and documentation defines the goal as creating/fixing skills so they can be reused next time and future conversations improve. - Adaptation option generated: patch a defective skill, encode an emergent workflow as a new reusable skill, consolidate durable knowledge/memory, complete suitable unfinished work, or deliberately make no change. - Path back into current capability / S3: committed workspace/skill/knowledge changes are the same first-party artifacts read by subsequent normal Agent operation; operator rollback remains a separate parent intervention path rather than the owner of the autonomous adaptation judgment.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: A(P) - Function: govern the Agent&#39;s durable identity/personality and highest-level workspace behavioral principles through first-party identity files that are loaded into subsequent system prompts. - Disturbance / variety regulated: drift between the Agent&#39;s durable identity/behavioral principles and explicit repeated signals about what the assistant should be, plus explicit operator decisions to redefine those identity/policy artifacts. - Decisive decision or feedback right: in the autonomous mode, decide whether an explicit repeated identity/personality/style signal is strong enough to justify a bounded AGENT.md identity revision; in the parent mode, authoritatively edit AGENT.md/RULE.md through the authenticated core-file administration surface. - Decision owner: base mode — the isolated Self-Evolution review Agent for the deliberately narrow autonomous identity-evolution decision; parent mode — the authenticated self-hosted operator as ultimate authority over the installation&#39;s core identity/policy files. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: evolution signal criteria and guarded writes/backups; AgentAdminService core-file revision checks and atomic writes; authenticated Web endpoint; prompt/workspace loader; targeted runtime eviction after operator edits. - Closure path: autonomous mode: repeated identity-level external signal → review-Agent identity judgment → guarded AGENT.md edit → subsequent runtime loads the changed persona. Parent mode: authenticated operator reads/edits a core identity/policy file → atomic persistence and cached-Agent eviction → subsequent Agent instance reloads that file into its system prompt. - Boundary reachability: both modes are shipped supported runtime paths: default-enabled Self-Evolution may edit AGENT.md under its standard guard, while the authenticated Web Agents API exposes core-file read/write and immediately evicts the changed runtime. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the base identity change is not a static prompt or automatic parser; the review Agent must judge whether a rare explicit repeated signal warrants changing its own identity artifact. The separate operator mode retains parent authority to set or override the same identity/policy surface, hence A(P) rather than plain A. - Evidence: pinned agent/evolution/prompts.py, executor.py, agent/prompt/workspace.py, agent/prompt/builder.py, agent/admin.py and channel/web/api/agents.py. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: ordinary permission settings, tool approvals, memory/preferences and per-task steering are not credited as S5. The autonomous claim is narrowly limited to the explicit first-party AGENT.md identity-evolution path; operator edits establish the separate parent mode. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: AGENT.md is explicitly defined by the shipped templates as the Agent&#39;s identity/personality and is loaded with RULE.md into the system-prompt context; this is materially different from ordinary task or tool policy. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: base (A) — Self-Evolution Agent decides whether the standard rare-signal criterion warrants a durable identity edit; parent (P) — authenticated operator directly owns the authoritative core-file edit and may override/undo Agent evolution. - Return-to-operation path: both modes persist the core file used by prompt construction; operator writes additionally evict the cached runtime, and later Agent initialization reloads the changed identity/policy context.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/a00011ae5e8e9ee62aa713d51f283559f12102d4/assessments/cowagent.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit a00011ae5e8e9ee62aa713d51f283559f12102d4\">a00011a &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-16T23:24:44+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/agno-agi/agno\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Agno</a><small>2022</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-15\">2026-09-15</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/agno-agi/agno/tree/44219f8532e2fe6ce936850455f4b9a2567e4194\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 44219f8532e2fe6ce936850455f4b9a2567e4194\">44219f8 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: member agents autonomously execute bounded model/tool work and return results.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: TeamMode.tasks provides an out-of-box agent-owned mutual-adjustment loop over a shared task board. The leader assigns ownership, prevents duplicate tasks, encodes dependencies, observes member outcomes and can retry/reassign/change the plan; dependency state prevents conflicting/premature execution. This goes beyond one-shot delegation.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: the same leader has a current whole-team view of tasks, assignees, dependency/blocked state, results and failures, plus authority to create/reprioritize through dependencies, assign/reassign members, execute work, update status and decide overall completion. That is a concrete agent-owned current-control loop over shared operational commitments. Human-in-the-loop paths pause member/tool execution and return the result to the team; they do not independently take ownership of the team-level assignment/plan/completion right, so no separate (P) mode is published.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: Agno lets applications compose reviewer/critic members, but the standard team runtime does not supply a distinct sufficiently independent audit channel with alternative operational access and required corrective closure.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: memory, knowledge, reasoning and learning capabilities can improve team operation but do not by themselves instantiate a distinct external-and-prospective intelligence function coupled against S3.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: team role, instructions, permissions, approvals and identity are application/parent configured. The tasks leader regulates execution but is not given ultimate agent-owned policy/identity authority or an S3–S4 closure role.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/9ee1ea7472777767928c8b2ab035e0948a0467e9/assessments/agno.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 9ee1ea7472777767928c8b2ab035e0948a0467e9\">9ee1ea7 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-18T03:22:19+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/google-research/harness2\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Harness²</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-18\">2026-10-18</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/google-research/harness2/tree/1cfe254e8d25b15f37048c3730134686abe60335\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 1cfe254e8d25b15f37048c3730134686abe60335\">1cfe254 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Carry out the present task&#39;s harness-editing and probing work through autonomous model-chosen candidate changes, actual executor rollouts and evidence-informed candidate selection. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unseen tasks, insufficient baseline harness behavior, different candidate tool/prompt/skill modifications, executable errors and heterogeneous task deliverables. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The configured proposer model chooses what edits to write inside the allowed harness tree; selector model chooses among concrete completed candidate outputs using execution evidence. Those chosen changes are validated and run by the shipped orchestration, with later candidate/edit choices exposed to resulting observations. - Decision owner: Model-backed Proposer/selector operating through first-party runstage, runmode and runselection; external model/runtime implementations supply inference and task execution but do not replace first-party decision/feedback routing. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Python stage scheduler, editable tree validation, candidate/run budget ledger, real adapter execution, retry/fallback, evidence-safe render, filesystem result records and selection persistence. - Closure path: Task/base rollout → proposal-agent harness edit → first-party tree accept/reject → substrate probe with new tree → observed deliverable/trajectory → independent composition/selection decision → chosen current-task output and selection record. - Boundary reachability: Public CLI runsweep → rundomain → runtask → runmode → runstage/real candidate adapter execution → runselection is shipped source. Proposer and selector may be model-backed via released Proposer.call implementations rather than sample-only DemoProposer. No external task executor cognitive function is claimed as a Harness² internal actor. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: First-party code gives model-backed editor/selector discretion that changes candidate harnesses and selects from observed task outcomes; deterministic scheduler, validator and external benchmark judge are only enforcement/evidence mechanisms. - Evidence: harness2/core/schedule.py; harness2/core/schedule.py; harness2/core/schedule.py; harness2/core/recursion.py; harness2/core/recursion.py; harness2/core/recursion.py; harness2/core/selection.py; harness2/core/proposer.py. - Basis: Reachable configured proposal/edit/probe/compare/select source loop for current task. - Confidence: High for structural mode reachability; no model-backed external benchmark execution attested. - Caveats: Model-less deterministic demo is not itself autonomous; the positive applies only to the supported configured model editor/selector with functional substrate. Absolute benchmark gains are not used as VSM classification evidence.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No independent S2 feedback mechanism stabilizes specific interference between separately viable S1 operating units at the declared recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Parallel task candidates and concurrent domains can compete for execution resources or diverge, but running k proposals/rollouts is controlled experimental scheduling rather than detected inter-S1 organizational conflict. - Decisive decision or feedback right: ThreadPoolExecutor widths, domain ordering, mode-separated namespaces and shared candidate-pool aggregation control concurrency/experiment identity. No agent makes inter-operating-division stabilization decisions that feed back to competing S1 units. - Decision owner: Deterministic task/domain scheduler and user-specified budgets/workers, not a model-owned S2 coordination actor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Candidate labels, per-mode Namespace, thread pools, deterministic domain order, cost ledger and output directory partitioning. - Closure path: Proposals/rollouts execute and join → composition/selection. Missing a sensed cross-S1 interference → specific adaptive damping judgment → returned adjustments to each affected autonomous S1 operating division. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Task stages and domain jobs are parts of one harness-optimization experiment; concurrent task execution and result aggregation do not automatically establish S2 among independent viable operating organizations. - Evidence: harness2/core/schedule.py; harness2/core/schedule.py; harness2/core/schedule.py; harness2/core/recursion.py; harness2/core/recursion.py; harness2/core/ledger.py. - Basis: Bounded source negative after identifying all shipped within-task and across-domain concurrency mechanisms. - Confidence: High for reviewed default modes. - Caveats: An external experiment orchestrator or multi-tenant platform could add S2, but neither is part of the assessed frozen first-party distribution.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No distinct whole-current organization control loop assigns and revises commitments/resources/priorities over multiple independent S1 operating domains. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Task failures, budgets and experimental queue pressure are managed at run scope; no whole-system operational obligations spanning independent current S1 divisions are established. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Scheduler selects the prespecified task order/mode/budget and handles per-task failures; model selector chooses a task artifact; neither makes a global current-control intervention among distinct operating units. - Decision owner: CLI operator for configuration, deterministic Python scheduler for execution and model editor/selector for task results. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Namespace-separated runs, BudgetLedger, result statuses, resume, checked candidate trees and domain task streaming. - Closure path: Configured tasks → bounded execution/selection/summary. Missing whole-organization status → legitimate binding current priority/resource/commitment decision → intervention returned into separate S1 operations. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Select-best-harness for one task is S1; allocating worker count by human CLI is configuration. Neither is an autonomous or first-party S3 decision over a viable multi-unit current organization. - Evidence: harness2/core/schedule.py; harness2/core/schedule.py; harness2/core/schedule.py; harness2/core/schedule.py; harness2/core/ledger.py; harness2/core/selection.py. - Basis: Boundary-relative bounded negative separating experiment management from organization current control. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: Research lab/program management may be external; it is not an owned Harness² control function.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No independent complementary audit obtains separate operational reality evidence, challenges S3 assertions and returns authorized corrective S3 action. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Candidate harnesses can fail or produce inferior outcomes, and benchmark grader information could leak. First-party tree validation, contrastive probing and independent post-run judge help task-level evidence integrity but do not audit organizational S3 performance claims. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Code validates a tree and denies grader-verdict leakage; a selector judges delivered candidate outputs while an external rubric judge scores outputs in a separate stage. None reports independently sourced whole-current operating contradictions to a legitimate S3 manager with binding correction. - Decision owner: First-party task/evidence validator and choice-model for the current improvement task; external benchmark judge for scoring, not a separate audited management officer. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Editable-tree validator, forbidden-grader evidence checks, candidate pool anonymization, external judgeone, cost ledger and summary artifacts. - Closure path: Candidate output/trajectory → validation and selection/benchmark score → task/result record; missing S3 current report → independent operating observation → complementary challenge → authorized corrective management return. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Contrastive behavioral evidence and result-score separation are real, but output selection for one task is neither S3 evidence independence nor an audit owner with a corrective cross-system path. - Evidence: harness2/core/recursion.py; harness2/core/selection.py; harness2/core/selection.py; harness2/core/judge.py; harness2/core/context.py; harness2/core/ledger.py. - Basis: Bounded negative after explicitly considering the grader/selector/evidence-control positive candidate. - Confidence: Medium-high within the frozen app. - Caveats: A dedicated independently observable production system auditor could be built externally; no such shipped owner and returned S3 correction were shown.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: Learn from current encountered environments and adopted task-level harness modifications to autonomously maintain a reusable future-facing improvement library that influences subsequent harness-design decisions across new tasks. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Open-world tasks arrive that were not known at initial harness construction; improvements for one problem may not transfer directly. Cross-task domain patterns, changing successful component choices and observed errors must inform future harness proposals. - Decisive decision or feedback right: After actual current-task rollouts, the model-backed selector picks an adopted harness from candidate evidence and model-backed reflectadoption distills transferable experience. First-party Library.add accepts that model-selected result/lesson into durable per-domain library, and subsequent proposer/editor sessions read it for future harness reconfiguration. - Decision owner: Configured autonomous selector + experience-reflecting model agent inside the shipped runtask/rundomain loop. Deterministic file/library code persists and presents the agent&#39;s chosen strategic material, not an external human deciding each adopted lesson. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Domain task stream, mode-separated Library, selected harness tree and adoption metadata, contrastive trajectory record, EXPERIENCE.md and INDEX.md, context injection, safe tree validation, leak prevention and retaining only earlier tasks. - Closure path: Environment/task rollouts → model selection of accepted harness → model distills evidence-supported transferable future lesson → library persists full adopted harness + rationale → next task imports library into new model proposer context → its operational harness edits can change under accumulated past experience. - Boundary reachability: A first-party public runsweep invokes rundomain, creates Library objects per mode/domain and executes tasks sequentially. runonemode calls reflectadoption and, with evidence-rich experience, modelib.add; runstage passes librarydir to context.build which actually copies old library context into the next proposer session. No bespoke adopter glue is needed for this supported released mode. - External distinction: Novel tasks in the same domain arrive after prior harnesses have been accepted; actual task-execution contrasts, error patterns and domain-specific cues are fed back through the prior-task experience library rather than assumed from static program configuration. - Future / prospective distinction: The reflector distils a transferable cue/lesson intended for later unknown tasks, and Library stores adopted trees and why they were chosen; this is not the immediate selected deliverable alone. - Adaptation option generated: An autonomous proposer/editor generates new harness-component edits based on stored prior domain experience, and an autonomous selector/reflection loop chooses, explains and retains the successful candidate tree and lesson to inform later alternative edits. - Path back into current capability / S3: Library.add persists the chosen tree and lesson, context.build copies the resulting library into the next proposer work directory, and recursion.runstage hands it to the model edit session whose validated candidate modifies the next task&#39;s executable harness. There is no separate whole-current S3 manager, so the concrete return is direct into next S1 harness-improvement capability at this recursion. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The agent makes substantive adoption and transferable-experience decisions from observed external task outcomes; first-party memory storage/return closes them into future edit decisions. This is a prospective reusable improvement-harness capability, distinct from current task&#39;s best-output selection alone. - Evidence: harness2/core/schedule.py; harness2/core/schedule.py; harness2/core/schedule.py; harness2/core/selection.py; harness2/core/selection.py; harness2/core/selection.py; harness2/core/library.py; harness2/core/library.py; harness2/core/context.py; harness2/core/recursion.py. - Basis: Installed agent-owned adoption/reflection and persistent cross-task future-harness change feedback, independent of reported benchmark gains. - Confidence: Medium-high for the supported configured cross-task learning mode; effectiveness/generalization not independently tested. - Caveats: This is S4 of the declared harness-improvement organization, not S4 of a separately deployed fixed target coding assistant. Individual future tasks begin from a base harness but the stored adopted earlier trees and model lessons are available to the editing proposer; this is guidance-mediated capability adaptation, not unconditional promotion of a new globally deployed runtime. A failed or empty reflection prevents library admission, so not every task advances S4.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No ultimate legitimate mission/identity-policy arbitration for the harness-improvement organization is shipped as a first-party closed decision path. - Disturbance / variety regulated: User-selected benchmark, base model, allowed editable components, executor, task scope and no-leak policies bound optimization but do not entail an identity/purpose governance dispute. - Decisive decision or feedback right: CLI/research operator fixes the task stream, model/substrate budget and source boundaries; validators enforce prechosen component constraints. No first-party actor chooses legitimate ultimate aims or changes governing ethical purpose and returns those decisions. - Decision owner: External operator/research institution for mission and boundary setup; first-party deterministic config/validation has no identity-level authority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Benchmarks, CLI config, read-only rubric protection, editable-component allowlist, ledger and model/executor selection. - Closure path: Configured objective/limits → task improvements in those limits; missing legitimate ultimate policy/identity alternatives → authoritative S5 choice → returned system-wide changed governing purpose. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Technical safety and user objective configuration are narrower than governing what the system is and what its ultimate purpose should be. - Evidence: harness2/core/schedule.py; harness2/core/spec.py; harness2/core/tree.py; harness2/core/recursion.py; docs/EXPERIMENTS.md. - Basis: Bounded no-first-party identity-purpose governance after inspecting all admissibility and external authority surfaces. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: Google Research or the deployer may separately exercise organizational S5; not imported as a Harness² runtime ownership claim.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/9a20d73e3efcee2bcc1639c7365c3b178e155f74/assessments/harness2.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 9a20d73e3efcee2bcc1639c7365c3b178e155f74\">9a20d73 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-15T20:43:38+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Harvil1/codeAgent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CodeAgent (Harvil1)</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-15\">2026-10-15</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Harvil1/codeAgent/tree/e48780be52949dfcf17338025f6beab48ca01428\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision e48780be52949dfcf17338025f6beab48ca01428\">e48780b &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Select coding actions from changing repository observations and execute model-directed file/shell/tool work until task completion or bounded stop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: User coding requirements, file contents, tool success/failure, budgets, permission denials. - Decisive decision or feedback right: AIAgent&#39;s model selects tools and follow-up actions after receiving tool results; no human per-step selection required in allowed mode. - Decision owner: The packaged AIAgent/model-led loop; external provider performs inference but does not supply a separate task-owning agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Agent iteration budget and stop handling, permission gates, modeltools dispatch, sessions/context, tool adapters. - Closure path: runconversation → model request → dispatchtoolcalls → tool result/history → next runconversation iteration or final answer. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: AIAgent owns the decision/observation/continuation loop and can operate locally through the shipped CLI. A provider API alone would not produce this tool-control loop. - Boundary reachability: The supported cli.main / runinteractive instantiates AIAgent; the model-tool loop and tools are shipped first-party. - Evidence: agent/init.py; cli.py; modeltools.py; agent/llmclient.py. - Basis: explicit shipped control paths and structural source-first reconstruction at the frozen revision. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: This is static source evidence, not a live benchmark; scope is limited to first-party packaged operation.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: Attenuate specific duplicate assignment and unauthorized task-state interference among separately running child worker S1 units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Two independently running workers may attempt to claim/operate the same kanban task and corrupt shared task ownership. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Deterministic conditional task claim arbitrates ownership; coordinator reports contested-task failure to the caller, and binding refuses writes to another worker&#39;s task. - Decision owner: First-party constructed TaskStore/TeamCoordinator ownership guard; discretionary rescope after a rejection belongs to the parent model, not to the file lock. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Cross-process lock, conditional status/owner update, CODEAGENTKANBANTASK binding, tasktools ownership enforcement. - Closure path: Parent selects a worker/task ID → coordinator claims task atomically → conflicting claim returns failure to parent as tool result → later parent assignment can change; admitted worker&#39;s task mutations are bounded by binding. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: This is narrower than generic bus/routing: it directly regulates competing ownership of a shared task between independent worker processes with a returned failure signal. It does not prove general repository file-conflict resolution. - Boundary reachability: teamspawn with taskid and task updates are first-party registered team/task tools used by the shipped model loop. - Distinct S1 units: first-party separately running agent/team/worker.py child coding model loops, each possibly assigned a TaskStore task in one parent-managed team. - Inter-S1 disturbance: two workers or successive assignments targeting one mutable task can contend for a single task owner, causing duplicate task-state writes/commitments. - Attenuating coordination relation: TeamCoordinator invokes atomic TaskStore.claim before worker process startup; claim rejects a task owned by another worker and a bound worker cannot update a different task. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: a rejected teamspawn/claim returns failure as a tool result to the parent model which can choose a different task/worker on a later loop; task store owner and status are visible to subsequent workers. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the conditional owner transaction directly rejects a concrete inter-worker duplicate-claim disturbance and returns the rejection to the choosing parent; this is narrow task-ownership collision attenuation, not general file/worktree conflict handling. - Evidence: agent/taskstore.py; agent/team/coordinator.py; agent/team/taskbinding.py; tools/teamtool.py; tools/tasktools.py. - Basis: explicit shipped control paths and structural source-first reconstruction at the frozen revision. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: This is static source evidence, not a live benchmark; scope is limited to first-party packaged operation.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: Regulate the live roster of child coding operations and their current task commitments at the main-agent team boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Worker availability, completion/failure, occupied task ownership, finite team capacity and conflicting active commitments. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The parent model can inspect teammembers and task state and choose teamspawn, teamshutdown and task assignments during an active session. - Decision owner: The main AIAgent using shipped team/task tools; TeamCoordinator enforces member caps and process transitions but does not choose the plan. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Registry with member status/role, teammembers, task tools, spawn/shutdown, message bus, worker status updates. - Closure path: Live roster/task results enter the model&#39;s next turn → model chooses worker/task intervention → coordinator changes running processes / task owner → returned status informs subsequent turns. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Distinct from mere process manager: a model-operable whole-current team roster and intervention tools are wired into the same first-party feedback loop. This is a structural capability claim, not a measured autonomous scheduling benchmark. - Boundary reachability: Team tools are registered with the shipped agent&#39;s callable toolset, not a developer CI script or external scheduler. - Whole-system current view: teammembers returns status/role/PID of all first-party team workers, and the task store exposes assigned current work and ownership to the main agent. - Current-control decision scope: main model can choose which worker/task to spawn, inspect current membership, shut down running workers, and revise current task commitments; deterministic team size/depth ceilings enforce but do not choose those decisions. - Evidence: tools/teamtool.py; agent/team/coordinator.py; agent/team/worker.py; agent/init.py; tools/tasktools.py. - Basis: explicit shipped control paths and structural source-first reconstruction at the frozen revision. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: This is static source evidence, not a live benchmark; scope is limited to first-party packaged operation.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: Provide sufficiently independent operational reality inspection with corrective return to the whole-current S3 controller. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Child reports may be inaccurate, tool outputs incomplete, and operator state may drift. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No separately established organization-wide complementary audit judgment with corrective S3 return is demonstrated. - Decision owner: Child-output checks and optional aux-LLM review are supporting validators; a distinct S3 audit owner is not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Child-claim filesystem/TaskStore check, appendwarning, optional handoffreviewenabled, trace logs. - Closure path: Claim mismatch warnings can reach parent context, but the verifier is scoped to a single delegation and does not own a complementary assessment of current system regulation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Cross-check of declared files/tasks is real yet narrowly local; source does not demonstrate an independent audit of S3 operations or an audit-based current-control corrective obligation. - Evidence: agent/team/hallucinationcheck.py; tools/delegatechild.py; agent/trace.py; config.py. - Basis: explicit shipped control paths and structural source-first reconstruction at the frozen revision. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: This is static source evidence, not a live benchmark; scope is limited to first-party packaged operation.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: Monitor the external future environment and choose/provide prospective organizational adaptation to the current coding system. - Disturbance / variety regulated: New external coding environments, future model/tool needs, shifts in operating strategy. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No first-party whole-system external/prospective adaptive decision right is demonstrated. - Decision owner: Developer/user controls extensions and learning enablement; heuristic skill evolution operates on past in-session trace patterns. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Disabled-by-default skilllearning observer/evolver, skill storage, memory/compaction, workflow and hooks. - Closure path: When enabled, repeated past tool patterns can become local skill files; the source does not close external-future sensing → strategic selection → changed operational capacity as an S4 function. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: An auto-generated habit from task traces is retrospective task improvement; the user-enabled feature by itself does not establish environment-facing prospective S4 governance. - Evidence: agent/skilllearning/turnobserver.py; agent/skilllearning/evolver.py; agent/skilllearning/observer.py; config.py. - Basis: explicit shipped control paths and structural source-first reconstruction at the frozen revision. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: This is static source evidence, not a live benchmark; scope is limited to first-party packaged operation.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: Resolve legitimacy, ultimate identity and binding mission/policy choices of the harness organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Conflicting user goals, safety settings, credentials and authorization limits. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Operators set higher-level objective/configuration; ordinary command approvals and system prompt constraints do not resolve organization-wide identity disputes. - Decision owner: Operator/developer owns settings, permissions and scope outside assessed autonomy; shipped code enforces rather than closes ultimate policy. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Permission subsystem, command/secret guards, config settings and model tool allowlists. - Closure path: Approvals or denials affect local tool calls; no supported first-party policy-identity escalation and authoritative returned decision loop is evidenced. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Local tool-control policy and user acceptance are not themselves an operational S5 mission/policy closure. - Evidence: agent/permission.py; config.py; agent/init.py; tools/teamtool.py. - Basis: explicit shipped control paths and structural source-first reconstruction at the frozen revision. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: This is static source evidence, not a live benchmark; scope is limited to first-party packaged operation.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/8b7f3f939013ab310a253b2b144d5f8e2654644a/assessments/codeagent-harvil1.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 8b7f3f939013ab310a253b2b144d5f8e2654644a\">8b7f3f9 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-23T18:41:54+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/GustavoGutierrez/alisio\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Alisio</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-23\">2026-10-23</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/GustavoGutierrez/alisio/tree/4a3c9c21b9e005f7bf8ebcd256bee5de60dc2e48\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 4a3c9c21b9e005f7bf8ebcd256bee5de60dc2e48\">4a3c9c2 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: execute user-requested coding operations in the repository, including independent bounded child tasks. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unfamiliar code, errors, shell results, changing task requirements, permissions, provider output and child-task results. - Decisive decision or feedback right: model-backed primary and child actors select tools/arguments and adapt subsequent actions to tool output and instructions. - Decision owner: primary Alisio model agent and distinct subordinate model sessions in supported delegation mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: provider/session bindings, tool registry, permissions, active-agent selection, child session creation, depth/turn limits and tool-result persistence. - Closure path: model decides tool or child task → runtime executes or denies → tool output/child result enters that actor&#39;s or parent&#39;s conversation → agent selects further work. - Boundary reachability: CLI ships the core runner and enables the subagents plugin by default; its built-in general/explore/plan definitions instantiate separate child model sessions via registered tools. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model chooses work and responds to results; runner and permissions execute/constrain but do not preselect individual code edits. - Evidence: runner.ts, index.ts, manager.ts. - Basis: structural source and first-party operating documentation; native deterministic subagent tests. - Confidence: high for supported operating path. - Caveats: no connected live LLM was run; native tests corroborate constructor and control behavior, not model quality.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: dampen interference among independently deciding child coding S1s working under one primary agent. - Disturbance / variety regulated: concurrent write-capable children may overwrite one another&#39;s changes or generate conflicting repository modifications. - Distinct S1 units: two or more child model sessions with separate conversations and scoped tool rights, scheduled through the built-in task tool. - Inter-S1 disturbance: startTask explicitly detects at least two overlapping write-capable children and activates a collision-specific mitigation path; read-only work does not require writer isolation. - Attenuating coordination relation: the primary model selects child roles, bounds instructions and work decomposition and can adjust running children via sendmessage; the built-in manager then serializes writers or uses isolated git worktrees under the configured/operator-chosen policy. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: assigned child instructions and write boundaries govern the work they undertake; queued/finished results and worktree diffstats return to the parent agent, which can revise later delegation or message a child. Worktree changes require explicit integration before affecting the shared tree. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the separate mitigation is triggered by an implemented concurrent write collision class, and changes the execution location/ordering of precisely those S1s; it is not a generic task queue or chat transport alone. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the parent model decides which independent child work to run and its task-specific coordination boundaries, and can revise a child after result feedback; an interactive human separately chooses worktree/serial/shared collision policy when prompted. - Decision owner: model-backed primary actor for task-specific coordination; interactive operator for the write-safety policy choice; deterministic SubagentManager for enforcement only. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: 40 ms overlapping-writer detection, config parallelWrites, terminal selector, serial mutex, git-worktree setup, child results/diffstat, taskstatus and sendmessage. - Closure path: primary selects concurrent child roles/prompts → manager detects write contention and enforces selected isolation/serialization → child S1s work within resulting constraints → their results and branch changes return → primary revises future coordinated delegation. - Boundary reachability: default-enabled plugin registers the agent-owned task/sendmessage tools and ships built-in independently executing children plus a write-specific first-party safeguard. Headless runs use serial writes without requiring interactive selection. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removal of the primary actor leaves only enforcement of already selected collision policies, not task-specific decisions about which child work should coexist or how results should alter subsequent child instructions. The operator&#39;s independent choice of safety mode is not falsely attributed to the model. - Evidence: manager.ts, index.ts, docs/subagents.md, tests/subagents.test.ts. - Basis: explicit interference guard plus structural independent child-agent decision/feedback path. - Confidence: medium-high: S2 is bounded to one primary agent&#39;s cooperating child set, not a globally autonomous distributed coordinator. - Caveats: the default interactive write-safety mode is chosen by the human rather than the model; agent autonomy lies in task-specific child coordination and feedback, with code enforcing write isolation.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no separate whole-current system resource/commitment-control authority evidenced beyond parent task delegation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: child task capacity, deadlines and token limits exist, but the shipped scheduling layer enforces configured caps instead of making a whole-system discretionary commitment decision. - Decisive decision or feedback right: parent can launch/retry/message individual tasks; static runtime admits, queues, bounds or cancels sessions. - Decision owner: no evidenced owner of whole-current resource bargaining, organizational priority negotiation or cross-unit accountability at the assessed recursion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: per-parent/global concurrency caps, queue, session tree, token budgets, TUI cancel/merge commands and taskstatus. - Closure path: child work/reports reach parent and optional operator commands alter individual child execution; a separate whole-system resource/priority decision-return path is not established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: choosing an agent for a subtask and observing its progress is ordinary decomposition; structural limits do not give their enforcer discretion over present organization-wide commitments. - Evidence: manager.ts, config.ts, docs/subagents.md. - Basis: bounded first-party implementation inspection. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a custom primary prompt or outside orchestrator could exercise broader control, but it is not supplied by this standard runtime.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no independent operational-reality audit loop is wired to corrective control in the default product. - Disturbance / variety regulated: code errors can be tested or reviewed, but no complementary evidence-based challenge of normal child reports is installed. - Decisive decision or feedback right: a generic user-authored reviewer may read a diff, and output of ordinary tests returns to the producing agent; neither supplies first-party independent audit ownership. - Decision owner: no built-in S3 audit actor established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: child role definition loader, read-only permissions, Git tools, testing and result wrappers warning that child reports are not authoritative. - Closure path: ordinary tool/child results return to the primary operational path; no separately wired reviewer observation → audit judgment → corrective whole-control return. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a sample reviewer definition demonstrates composability but is not standard shipped audit closure or independent sampling of operational artifacts. - Evidence: definitions.ts, manager.ts, docs/subagents.md. - Basis: inspection of default agent catalog, delegation, returned results and optional reviewer example. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: downstream users can configure reviewers, but the assessed standard mode does not prescribe independent audit inputs, authority and returned corrective action.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no installed external/future-oriented capability adaptation loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: repeated session findings and environment information may be recorded, but prospective product redesign is not automatically selected or returned into the active harness. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the active model can plan its current task, while user/config controls memory/provider/agents; neither decides an S4 future capability change from environmental scanning. - Decision owner: no prospective organizational adaptation authority identified. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: @alisio/plugin-memory SQLite search and compaction, provider switches, skills, customizable agents, model planning. - Closure path: stored notes and retrieved context inform later coding requests; no environment horizon → adaptation options → selected durable capability change → current-control return is wired. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: generic memory and user-configured plugins facilitate S1 decisions, but do not establish an S4-specific option-selection/implementation owner. - Evidence: plugin-memory/src/index.ts, plugin-memory/src/store.ts, builtin.ts. - Basis: bounded memory/config/provider and runtime inspection. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a user&#39;s coding task may build future-facing software, but that is still S1 delivery without the organizational S4 loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no legitimate ultimate identity/policy adjudication with return to the coding organization is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: untrusted shell/files and unsafe parallel edits trigger permissions/coordination choices, not institutional purpose conflicts between current and future viability. - Decisive decision or feedback right: operator grants or refuses tool requests and chooses write-isolation posture; fixed config narrows sessions. - Decision owner: operator owns local permissions but no function-specific ultimate organizational S5 decision circuit is installed. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: trust settings, tool permission checks, child inheritance, bounded task scopes and interactive selection. - Closure path: action approval/denial alters one operation; no identity-level issue reaches a legitimate parent who decides ultimate policy and returns that decision as a governing rule to subsequent organization-wide operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a permission gate, project config or human merge command does not create ultimate identity/ethos authority. - Evidence: runner.ts, manager.ts, docs/subagents.md. - Basis: reviewed supported permissions, configuration and operator paths. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: product maintainers govern releases at an adjacent recursion, not the assessed running session.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/8e1f5f4555abcb899e02683ff0b8d7c2bbc74eed/assessments/al/alisio.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 8e1f5f4555abcb899e02683ff0b8d7c2bbc74eed\">8e1f5f4 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-01T22:13:35Z\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Nichonauta/MinAgent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">MinAgent</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-02\">2026-10-02</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Nichonauta/MinAgent/tree/02880e3c981ba57cd95d2266d6c0ea07b1abe44c\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 02880e3c981ba57cd95d2266d6c0ea07b1abe44c\">02880e3 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: transform a coding request into repository/process work or a task answer through model-selected actions with returned environment feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: repository/file state, implementation alternatives, tool and process results, endpoint uncertainty, context pressure, workspace guidance and user corrections. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next substantive coding/tool action and revise subsequent choices from returned environment evidence. - Decision owner: the configured model actor inside the first-party MinAgent conversation loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tool dispatcher, workspace confinement and write verification, terminal/MCP approval gates, context compaction, token/tool-round limits, skills/MCP adapters and streaming client. - Closure path: user objective/current context → model chooses tool call or answer → first-party MinAgent executes the permitted action → actual result is appended as a tool message → the same model receives that result and chooses another action or final response. - Boundary reachability: the documented minagent/node src/minagent.mjs entrypoint directly instantiates this shipped loop; built-in file tools and optional terminal/skills/MCP tools are presented through that same standard runtime. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: if the model actor is removed while tool schemas, workspace protections, limits and approvals remain, the runtime can enforce preselected constraints but does not choose task-specific coding actions. The substantive operational discretion therefore belongs to the model actor. - Evidence: README.md; src/minagent.mjs; src/workspace.mjs. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: model inference occurs through an external compatible endpoint, but first-party MinAgent closes its decisions through the workspace/tool feedback loop.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material inter-S1 disturbance-attenuation function is supplied at the assessed recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: no interaction-generated conflict or oscillation among multiple operational S1 units is established because the standard MinAgent composition exposes one primary operational agent loop. - Distinct S1 units: not established. Built-in tools, shell commands, MCP servers and skills are capabilities/dependencies used by the one operating agent rather than first-party autonomous operational units with their own durable local outcome/autonomy at this recursion. - Inter-S1 disturbance: not established. - Attenuating coordination relation: not established. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: tool results feed the same S1&#39;s next decision, not a coordination outcome among distinct S1s. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: it is not; the inspected paths are tool invocation, request sequencing and context transport inside one operational loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for S2. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: sequential tool-round loop, tool-call count limits, MCP/skill dispatch and process/file execution. - Closure path: not applicable for the negative finding. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model owns one S1&#39;s next action, but there is no separate coordination function over multiple S1s to own. - Evidence: README.md; src/minagent.mjs. - Basis: explicit + structural negative search. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: downstream users can connect external multi-agent MCP systems, but adjacent external compositions do not create first-party S2 for MinAgent itself.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no separate whole-system current-control function is established above the single operating MinAgent S1. - Disturbance / variety regulated: context pressure, tool limits, terminal permissions and workspace boundaries are regulated locally, but there is no population-level current resource/commitment control on behalf of a larger operational whole. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for S3. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: token/context budgets, automatic compaction, tool-round limits, terminal/MCP approval, process cleanup and workspace safety checks. - Closure path: not applicable for the negative finding. - Whole-system current view: MinAgent can estimate its own context, refresh its own workspace snapshot and track its own conversation/tool state; this is the current state of one S1 task, not a whole-system view over several autonomous operational commitments. - Current-control decision scope: deterministic limits and user approvals constrain one session. No separate actor allocates resources, priorities, commitments or interventions across autonomous S1 units. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model regulates its coding actions as S1, while deterministic runtime mechanisms regulate service/context bounds. Neither establishes a distinct S3 function at the chosen recursion. - Evidence: README.md; src/minagent.mjs. - Basis: explicit + structural negative search. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: current-state visibility is real but remains local operational state rather than metasystemic whole-current control.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no material complementary operational-audit loop with sufficiently independent access and returned corrective control is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: workspace operations use strong inline validation, but the standard runtime does not supply a separate complementary observer that can challenge ordinary S1 claims through materially different access. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for S3. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: atomic workspace operations, post-write reread/byte comparison, path identity checks, ordinary tool results and tests outside the assessed runtime. - Closure path: not applicable; inline execution validation returns the result of the same operation, not an independent audit finding into organizational control. - Claim being audited: no distinct operational claim is assigned to a complementary auditor. - Ordinary reporting path: built-in tool execution returns actual file/process results as tool messages to the operating model. - Complementary access path: not established. Post-write verification rereads the just-written target as part of the same first-party tool operation; it does not constitute alternative, sporadic or organizationally independent access beyond the normal production path. - Independence boundary: none qualifying in the standard distribution. External MCP services or developer tests are adjacent unless specifically composed into the runtime and do not establish first-party audit ownership here. - Who acts on findings: ordinary tool errors/results return to the operating model as S1 feedback; there is no distinct S3 finding/return path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model can react to tool failures, while deterministic file validation supports safe execution. No separate agent owns a complementary audit judgment. - Evidence: README.md; src/workspace.mjs; src/minagent.mjs. - Basis: explicit + structural negative search. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: strong verification is credited as operational safety evidence, not promoted to S3 without complementary independence.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: model the current project/workspace environment and turn those distinctions into durable project-specific guidance that changes later MinAgent operation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: variation in repository architecture, important directories, build/test commands, conventions, checks and stale or missing project guidance that would otherwise force later coding turns to rediscover project-specific operating knowledge. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide what project distinctions matter and what durable architecture/command/convention/check guidance should be written into the root AGENTS.md for subsequent operation. - Decision owner: the configured model actor invoked by MinAgent&#39;s first-party /init path. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: deterministic project-file candidate scoring/selection, workspace inventory, bounded file excerpts, secret redaction, atomic workspace write, workspace refresh and automatic AGENTS.md reload before model requests. - Closure path: /init invocation → first-party inventory and selected project evidence → model develops project-specific future operating guidance → MinAgent writes/updates root AGENTS.md → workspace refresh reloads that guidance → subsequent S1 model requests include it in the system prompt and can operate differently under the returned adaptation. - Boundary reachability: /init [focus] is a documented built-in command in the standard interactive runtime; its implementation calls collectProjectEssentials, invokes the configured model, writes AGENTS.md, and refreshes the same first-party workspace context used by later requests. - External distinction: repository/workspace evidence outside the harness itself, including project architecture, important files, commands, conventions and checks selected from the current project. - Future / prospective distinction: the generated guidance explicitly captures project knowledge intended to shape later coding work rather than merely answer the current request; stale existing guidance is to be corrected for subsequent use. - Adaptation option generated: the model selects and synthesizes the complete project-specific AGENTS.md content from the observed evidence, including which architecture facts, commands, conventions and checks should become durable guidance. - Path back into current capability / S3: MinAgent persists the selected adaptation as root AGENTS.md; refreshWorkspaceSnapshot loads it, and workspace inventory reloads AGENTS.md before every later model request into the system prompt. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the user triggers /init and may optionally supply focus, but deterministic selection/writing machinery does not determine the substantive project guidance. If the model actor is removed, the runtime still has an inventory and writer but no autonomous decision over what durable adaptation to synthesize from project evidence. The decisive adaptation judgment is therefore model-owned. - Evidence: README.md; src/minagent.mjs; src/init-project.mjs; src/workspace.mjs. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: /init is user-invoked rather than continuously scheduled, but invocation is not the decisive adaptation judgment. No distinct parent-governed S4 mode is claimed merely because a human can invoke or manually edit project guidance.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material runtime identity/ultimate-policy authority loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: system prompts, AGENTS.md, tool/workspace boundaries, terminal mode and MCP approval constrain operation, but the strongest policy premises are externally configured or belong to ordinary task/tool governance rather than runtime identity-level adjudication. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for S5. - Decision owner: user/operator and static first-party configuration for the strongest candidate policy constraints; the model owns S1 task decisions and S4 project-guidance synthesis, not ultimate organizational identity/policy. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: base system prompt, AGENTS.md, workspace boundary, TERMINALMODE, MCP approval, configuration validation, tool-call/round limits and skills/MCP enablement flags. - Closure path: not applicable for the negative finding; constraints are loaded/enforced or per-call approval is requested, but no identity/ultimate-policy issue is adjudicated by a legitimate runtime ultimate authority and returned as a revised governing premise. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: no first-party standard path frames a genuine MinAgent identity or ultimate-policy dispute for runtime resolution. /init adapts project-operating guidance and therefore maps to S4, not S5. - Ultimate authority: user/operator and shipped/configured constraints for tool permissions and runtime setup; no autonomous ultimate-policy actor is established. - Return-to-operation path: configured modes and approvals immediately affect ordinary tool execution, and generated AGENTS.md affects later work, but these paths do not carry an identity/ultimate-policy decision. The latter is already credited to project adaptation under S4. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model cannot autonomously redefine the runtime&#39;s ultimate workspace/approval/configuration premises through a first-party identity-governance loop. Removing the human/config sources leaves enforcement machinery but not an S5 decision process. - Evidence: README.md; src/minagent.mjs; src/config.mjs; src/workspace.mjs. - Basis: explicit + structural negative search. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: durable project guidance is not treated as identity merely because it is loaded into the system prompt; its documented purpose is project-specific future operating knowledge.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/57741270a63fffc0db0655d9ba05455aa737c7e7/assessments/minagent.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 57741270a63fffc0db0655d9ba05455aa737c7e7\">5774127 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-28T16:21:02Z\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/polyphemus-ai/release-rehearsal\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Polyphemus</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-28\">2026-09-28</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/polyphemus-ai/release-rehearsal/tree/02020f3bc2829c350fd726273a7721254068925f\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 02020f3bc2829c350fd726273a7721254068925f\">02020f3 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform substantive project work through model-driven reasoning, tool use and iterative response to environment/tool feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: ambiguous task requests, changing project/repository state, tool results and failures, external service responses, model uncertainty, provider/tool differences and local implementation/research variety. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next task-specific reasoning/tool action and revise later actions from returned observations in order to complete the assigned outcome. - Decision owner: the model-driven agent actor executed through the first-party Polyphemus session/workflow runtime. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: runTurn, provider adapters, model routing/fallback, tool registry, approvals, redaction, isolation workers, session persistence, skills/persona/project context and connection grants. - Closure path: user/workflow outcome + project/agent context → model actor selects an action/tool call → Polyphemus executes it within the allowed boundary → tool/environment result returns to the model → subsequent model action changes until the outcome/turn closes. - Boundary reachability: ordinary polyphemus sessions and workflow agent nodes directly invoke the shipped first-party loop and tools; no adjacent dogfood or contributor actor is required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model-driven actor while keeping routing, tools, isolation and persistence leaves execution machinery but removes the task-specific discretionary action choice. - Evidence: README.md; packages/core/src/loop.ts; packages/core/src/projects.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: model/provider internals and vendor CLI internals remain external dependencies. A credits the model actor reached through the first-party supported runtime, not organizational functions internal to those providers.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate a concrete interference mode created when distinct S1 agent runtimes work concurrently in one shared thread while preserving each runtime&#39;s coherent model-visible history. - Disturbance / variety regulated: concurrently working agents interleave messages in the shared thread; another agent&#39;s messages can appear between a tool call and its tool result, and a runtime can otherwise continue from a private history that omits intervening peer contributions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: determine the safe model-visible ordering/synchronization of shared-thread messages so each runtime sees its tool call/result pairs coherently while still incorporating peer contributions. - Decision owner: first-party deterministic Polyphemus runtime policy (stitchHistory plus shared-thread resynchronization); no autonomous agent owns the coordination semantics. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: per-agent aside runtimes, addressed-agent dispatch, shared thread store, per-agent stop state, held-message queue, sequence tracking and runtime/session persistence. - Closure path: concurrent S1 runtimes append interleaved shared-thread events → Polyphemus reconstructs model-valid history and refreshes the shared thread before subsequent turns → each affected agent receives the repaired/current conversation → later S1 behavior proceeds from coordinated state rather than the destructive interleaving/stale copy. - Boundary reachability: concurrent addressed agents and history stitching are shipped daemon/core behavior documented and implemented at the pinned revision; adopters do not have to invent a separate coordination protocol. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing either model actor does not remove the deterministic coordination policy; the same first-party runtime rule decides how interleaved shared history is repaired. The S2-specific path is therefore constructor/runtime-owned C, not agent-owned A. - Distinct S1 units: two or more independently addressed model-driven agent runtimes working concurrently in the same Polyphemus thread. - Inter-S1 disturbance: peer output interleaves with another agent&#39;s tool call/result sequence and can leave a model-facing history invalid or stale with respect to the shared conversation. - Attenuating coordination relation: stitchHistory moves tool results next to their calls, synthesizes a stopped-turn result where needed and preserves intervening peer material after the repaired pair; the multi-agent runtime also re-synchronizes each agent with shared thread state before later work. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: the repaired/current history is what the next model invocation receives, so subsequent reasoning/actions are conditioned on coordinated peer state rather than corrupted or permanently skipped context. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the positive mapping rests on an explicitly documented concurrency-induced model-history corruption/staleness mode and a shipped attenuation transform that changes later sibling-agent input. Addressing, queueing and shared storage alone are not used as the S2 witness. - Evidence: docs/design/parallel-agents.md; packages/core/src/history.ts; packages/daemon/src/server.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the held-message queue and agent addressing are generic routing/sequencing mechanisms by themselves. The positive S2 finding is narrower: concurrent shared-thread history interference plus the specific repair/resynchronization path.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party S3 function is established at the assessed project/deployment recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: per-run failures, step state, budgets, provider exhaustion, approvals and concurrent job status are regulated locally, but the reviewed boundary does not establish a separate whole-project current regulator with authority over the portfolio of S1 resources/commitments/priorities. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no qualifying whole-system current-control decision right is established. - Decision owner: not established for S3. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: durable run store, node state machine, retries, hard attempt/loop/budget limits, one-at-a-time workflow keys, provider fallback/capacity readings, session stop controls, scheduler and human gates. - Closure path: not applicable for the negative finding. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model nodes make local operational decisions, while the workflow engine deterministically advances fixed graphs and enforces preselected limits. Ordinary operator gates approve specific actions. Neither path demonstrates a separate agent-owned or constructor-specific whole-system current regulator at the selected recursion. - Evidence: docs/design/workflows.md; packages/core/src/workflows/define.ts; packages/daemon/src/runs.ts; packages/core/src/routing.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural negative search. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: at a narrower workflow-run recursion the deterministic engine strongly regulates execution, and a future portfolio-level scheduler/manager could change this assessment. The current conclusion is specific to the declared project/deployment recursion and Profile threshold.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge a produced software change using access and judgment distinct from the builder&#39;s ordinary production path, then return findings into correction before merge. - Disturbance / variety regulated: implementation defects, missing requirements, risky changes or false confidence that can survive the producing agent&#39;s own reasoning and objective checks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: inspect the exact candidate and decide whether to approve it or request changes based on direct repository/diff/test evidence. - Decision owner: the read-only model-driven Reviewer agent instantiated by the first-party ship-issue workflow; the workflow preferentially selects a different model vendor from the producing agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: fresh node session; independentOf provider selection; read-only mode; exact-head push/check linkage; typed review artifact; deterministic verdict parsing; GitHub Reviewer identity; exact-head posted review; merge guard. - Closure path: builder candidate + objective checks at exact head → separate reviewer agent directly inspects diff/files and runs relevant checks → reviewer returns approve or requestchanges with concerns → deterministic workflow either passes the approval check or loops findings back to the work node → repaired candidate is rechecked/re-reviewed before merge. - Boundary reachability: ship-issue is a built-in shipped workflow; its review node is explicitly readOnly and independentOf the producing node, and the run engine implements other-vendor reviewer selection in ordinary workflow execution. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the audit judgment about defects/adequacy comes from the separate reviewer model actor. Exact-head validation, read-only restrictions and merge gating enforce that judgment but do not decide the review verdict. - Claim being audited: that the candidate change at the exact pushed head satisfies the requested issue/plan and is safe/complete enough to merge. - Ordinary reporting path: the builder&#39;s submitted change report plus objective build/typecheck/lint/test/preview checks and workflow artifacts. - Complementary access path: a separate read-only reviewer session reads the actual diff/files and runs its own relevant checks against the candidate; it is not limited to the builder&#39;s self-report. - Independence boundary: the review node is declared independentOf the producing work node; the run engine attempts to choose a ready model from another vendor, and the bundled Reviewer is instructed not to edit/merge the candidate it audits. - Who acts on findings: the workflow&#39;s approval check and repair loop return requestchanges concerns to the producing work node; subsequent push/check/review repeats on the corrected head. Merge additionally verifies an approval by an identity other than author/merger at the exact head. - Evidence: docs/design/workflows.md; packages/core/src/workflows/ship.ts; packages/core/src/workflows/define.ts; packages/daemon/src/runs.ts; packages/core/templates/agents/reviewer/instructions.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: when no ready model from another vendor exists, the run engine can fall back to the same vendor and records that loss of vendor diversity. The positive state relies on the standard supported mode in which independent reviewer selection is available and on role/session/read-only separation plus direct artifact access, not on vendor diversity alone.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material external-and-prospective adaptation loop is established at the assessed project/deployment recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: provider capacity, project history, current external events and scheduled triggers are observed, but the standard distribution does not convert a model of changing external/future conditions into adaptation options that return through S3 to change present organizational capability. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no qualifying prospective adaptation judgment/feedback right is established. - Decision owner: not established for S4. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: provider usage forecasting, fallback routing, project memory/notes/handoff, orientation, routines/triggers, web/external connections and editable project/agent configuration. - Closure path: not applicable for the negative finding. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: agents can research, react to external events and propose memory/rules, and runtime code can forecast quota exhaustion, but these paths remain current task execution, memory/governance or resource routing rather than a distinct outside-and-then adaptation function. - Evidence: packages/core/src/forecast.ts; packages/core/src/routing.ts; packages/core/src/projects.ts; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural negative search. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: general-purpose agents can of course be tasked with future research, and custom workflows could be authored. Generic expressiveness does not establish a first-party S4 path under the Methodology.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Function: maintain durable project identity and ultimate project ground rules through an authoritative parent-owned rule surface that governs later agent operation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: proposed changes to what the project is, how its work should be done and which durable rules/conventions future agents must follow. - Decisive decision or feedback right: accept or reject a proposed authoritative project-rule change (AGENTS.md) before it becomes the rule set future project sessions receive. - Decision owner: the legitimate self-hosted project operator/user in the first-party review flow. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: project scaffold; orientation agent; inbox; poly projects review / app Review UI; resolveInboxItem; project briefing/session context injection; agent/vendor support for AGENTS.md. - Closure path: current project/context or orientation agent surfaces a proposed rule/identity update into the project inbox → operator keeps or discards it → a kept AGENTS.md replaces the authoritative project rule file → later project sessions load/follow the changed rules. - Boundary reachability: project creation/orientation/review and per-session project briefing are shipped first-party product paths at the pinned revision; no contributor-governance or development-only actor is needed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: agents can draft/propose durable rules but are explicitly prevented from making them authoritative directly. The parent operator owns the ultimate keep/discard decision; therefore the established mode is P, not A. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: the durable declaration of what the project is, how work is done and the ground rules all project agents must follow, rather than an approval of one ordinary tool call or merge. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: parent mode only — the legitimate local project operator/user accepting or rejecting the proposed authoritative AGENTS.md change. - Return-to-operation path: accepted rule file becomes the project&#39;s authoritative AGENTS.md; subsequent Polyphemus sessions in that project receive/read those rules and conduct later work under them. - Evidence: docs/design/projects.md; packages/core/src/projects.ts; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: ordinary tool approvals, merge gates and model-fallback questions are not used as S5 evidence. The positive mapping is limited to durable project identity/ground-rule governance and its returned effect on future sessions.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/669e24a89f6ea047486bf56febc9a2ed94b72904/assessments/polyphemus.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 669e24a89f6ea047486bf56febc9a2ed94b72904\">669e24a &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-23T02:22:41+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/AadiXC0DE/Rune\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Rune</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-23\">2026-10-23</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/AadiXC0DE/Rune/tree/538f8bdb96c926880628c102fdc2cd8c2d473fb1\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 538f8bdb96c926880628c102fdc2cd8c2d473fb1\">538f8bd &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: choose and perform bounded repository investigation, editing, shell/verification actions and response revision for a user&#39;s current coding task. - Disturbance / variety regulated: uncertain repository layout, tool failures, unexpected file contents, user steering and incomplete verification. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the active model selects tool calls, interprets returned results and chooses the next coding step or completion; the user and fixed policy retain higher authority. - Decision owner: model actor inside the native interactive session or one-shot task. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: turn::runturn, advertised tool registry, workspace context, result/event recording, budget and policy checks. - Closure path: user request → model-generated tool call → authorized tool result → history/steering feedback → next model action or final response. - Boundary reachability: rune interactive session directly calls turn::runturn with production registry; this does not depend on the dormant child bridge. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model chooses task-level investigation and correction, whereas deterministic sandbox, limits and permission rules only bound those choices. - Evidence: turn execution loop, native session. - Basis: direct in-distribution model/tool action-and-feedback closure. - Confidence: high for code-path structure, untested task effectiveness. - Caveats: configured model service required at actual runtime; no live LLM performance measurement.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: construct bounded same-parent coordination/admission among separately identified child S1 workers, avoiding duplicate identity and exhaustion of the available child slots. - Disturbance / variety regulated: overlapping child identities that make feedback ambiguous, multiple attempted child commitments against the same finite parent budget, and stale commands after a child has ended. - Decisive decision or feedback right: first-party Registry::create rejects duplicate active named children and excess children; feedback channels reject closed/full queues and only drain guidance at step boundaries, returning errors to the caller. - Decision owner: fixed first-party per-parent admission/feedback rule; a host-composed parent model would have to decide how to respond to rejection. No autonomous S2 owner is shipped in the native CLI. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ChildKind::Named, Registry, Feedback::queue/drain, Subagent::new(Delegate, Authority), authority inheritance and revocation checks. - Closure path: two potential child S1 commitments contend for the same parent name/slot → the library constructor rejects interference and exposes the failure → composed parent can redirect or wait → later eligible child request can proceed. The host must supply the actual child runner and complete decision loop. - Boundary reachability: the public rune-agent Registry/Subagent types are first-party constructor exports; live child operation requires host composition, while the standard CLI deliberately registers Unsupported and is not a positive S2 runtime witness. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: an S2-specific admission relation exists at the constructor level, but its admission decision is deterministic; no shipped autonomous coordinator controls multiple live children. - Distinct S1 units: separate prospective registered child sessions under one parent, each with its own task brief and child identity after a host supplies Delegate. - Inter-S1 disturbance: two children with an identical addressable name would compete for the same parent messaging target, or excessive parallel child commitments would exceed the parent&#39;s declared capacity. - Attenuating coordination relation: reject duplicate names, enforce per-parent child ceiling, validate feedback queue validity and return explicit failures. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: constructor errors and bounded feedback returns let the parent change the next child request or delay it; queued steering is drained at a child boundary when supplied by a host. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: checks are scoped to competing sibling agent identities and their shared per-parent execution budget, not a generic message transport alone. - Evidence: child registry and feedback, delegate constructor, native Unsupported wiring. - Basis: function-specific constructor, not delivered autonomous CLI coordination. - Confidence: moderate; precise structural constructor, no live multi-child host proved. - Caveats: no workspace conflict resolver, scheduling policy or independent sibling agents in the standard CLI; do not upgrade this state to A on the strength of uncompiled Bridge source.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: choose and revise the current local task&#39;s overall commitments and completion order, conditional on results, within a single running session. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing current task scope, diverging steps, unsuccessful verification, available token/step budget and mid-turn user correction. - Decisive decision or feedback right: model can replace its entire live task plan (pending/inprogress/completed) and choose whether to inspect, patch, verify, retry or return a bounded partial outcome. - Decision owner: session-level coding model; user can steer individual decisions but the positive claim does not rely on a separate autonomous child hierarchy. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: updateplan whole-snapshot, per-turn history/steering, one-active-step consistency check, visible state events and deterministic limits. - Closure path: task goal and current plan → model observes operational results or queued guidance → revises overall near-term plan and work priority → calls ensuing tools or returns to user. - Boundary reachability: updateplan is supplied in the native tools inventory and turn::runturn handles resulting plan events in ordinary sessions; no external controller is needed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: selecting and revising current work priorities is model discretion; enforcing the maximum number of plan steps or the context budget is not itself S3 ownership. - Whole-system current view: within the deliberately narrow one-task recursion, the model sees task history, one live plan snapshot and returned tool outcomes, not a fleet-wide multi-agent portfolio. - Current-control decision scope: order/replace all current task commitments, move a different step to active status, reconsider remaining work on failure and decide completion/reporting of the local coding task. - Evidence: bounded plan replacement, turn history and steering, native tool preparation. - Basis: local-current control over the entire bounded task, not organization-wide resource allocation. - Confidence: moderate; model option present, actual fidelity not run. - Caveats: the review finds no active native child portfolio and does not credit speculative parent-wide coordination or an independent parent-mode S3 controller from mere CLI stop/steer.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: independently challenge the S3 controller&#39;s account of live current-task management using a separate evidentiary route, then return management correction. - Disturbance / variety regulated: erroneous priority/commitment accounting or uncorrected S3 narrative; no such closed complementary audit is established. - Decisive decision or feedback right: absent. rune review takes a provided pending diff and invokes ordinary ask; automatic review decides whether an unresolved tool action is safe, not whether S3 is reporting management reality faithfully. - Decision owner: no in-boundary complementary S3 audit owner identified. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: diff capture, per-action model review/hold, ordinary user-visible session log. - Closure path: neither diff feedback nor per-action permission review is an independent S3 claim → direct evidence → corrective control path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a second review model is not S3 simply because it exists; the audited claim and corrective loop are about another function. - Evidence: one-shot diff review, action reviewer, permission routing. - Basis: bounded negative at the declared local-task management recursion. - Confidence: high for two checked reviewer paths. - Caveats: external ACP clients may independently audit S3, but those client arrangements are not shipped Rune ownership.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: learn from outside/future distinctions and decide changes to reusable future task capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: new project/tool environments, evolving methods or recurrent skill failures; no first-party prospective adaptation closure found. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established; static skills and layered instruction files are loaded, not autonomously revised from external research or operational outcomes. - Decision owner: no credited S4 owner; human maintainers can edit project/user instructions outside the assessed running harness. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AGENTS.md scope chain, skills catalog, prompt assembly and persisted logs/commands. - Closure path: prompt/context injection affects a present run; it is not a demonstrated future-option generation and return path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: current context search, task re-planning and manually edited files do not alone imply outside-and-then intelligence. - Evidence: instructions discovery, prompt assembly, configuration. - Basis: bounded negative for first-party future adaptation. - Confidence: moderate-high. - Caveats: maintainers&#39; NEXT-PHASE.md is a roadmap, not an adaptive operating function.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Function: set the standing owner-approved normative boundary for the local assistant&#39;s identity, provider, permissions and allowed repository operations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: attempts by untrusted repository configuration or a model action to override the user&#39;s provider/permission identity, authorization and tool-safety policy. - Decisive decision or feedback right: user selects profile-owned provider, permission mode, rule settings and secrets; the project .rune.toml cannot override these keys; operator CLI/environment overrides remain explicit outside-model controls. - Decision owner: legitimate local human/host operator (P), not the task model or untrusted repository contributor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: five-layer config resolver with project-safe whitelist, PermissionMode, rule/approval routing, runtime tool-call gate and inherited child authority caps. - Closure path: operator configures durable user policy → project-owned unsafe keys are ignored with diagnostics → running host resolves and enforces effective policy on attempted actions → subsequent work stays under that operator boundary. - Boundary reachability: the native rune entry loads these settings before interactive or ask execution and applies permission mode during live tool-call decisions. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: setting ultimate provider/permission identity belongs to the local operator; model/automatic reviewer judges an individual unresolved action within this standing policy and cannot rewrite its authority. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: whose standing safety, provider and authorization configuration may govern later local assistant sessions, including when repository contents disagree. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: user/host operator (P) alone; no agent-owned ultimate identity mode established. - Return-to-operation path: resolved Settings selects provider, working mode and permission decision path for later agent tool calls. - Evidence: config separation and precedence, project key restriction, native run settings, policy gate. - Basis: standing identity/ultimate-policy authority returning to real operation, not a per-call permission prompt alone. - Confidence: moderate-high. - Caveats: this is a local installation&#39;s policy recursion; neither OSS contributors nor model provider are a single distributed S5 parent for all installations.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/3aeedb490ca2710f836de267b35e0ea765b0571c/assessments/aa/aadixc0de-rune.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 3aeedb490ca2710f836de267b35e0ea765b0571c\">3aeedb4 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-23T21:16:13+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/EthanYin-0701/sangxia-ai\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Sangxia.ai</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-23\">2026-10-23</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/EthanYin-0701/sangxia-ai/tree/f38f484d8c7f15f6937d5da553d9c25ef1953daf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision f38f484d8c7f15f6937d5da553d9c25ef1953daf\">f38f484 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: interpret a developer request, inspect/edit files, invoke tools and return a coding outcome. - Disturbance / variety regulated: variable repository structure, requested edits, tool errors, model errors, path/permission constraints and interrupted sessions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select next tool calls and arguments in response to observations and tool results; decide whether further coding iterations are needed. - Decision owner: the configured model actor in runTurn, subject to permission/timeout/hook gates. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ACP session management, tool registry, input-schema validation, permission prompts, bash/file tools, MCP integration, streamed provider calls, bounded retries and event persistence. - Closure path: user task → model choice → validated and permitted local tool call → observable tool result in conversation history → model&#39;s next work or final answer. - Boundary reachability: standard ACP and local TUI sessions both call SangxiaAgent.prompt, which invokes the shipped model/tool loop with first-party tools. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic dispatch enforces calls, but the model chooses operational actions based on returned evidence. The operator can veto protected effects without owning the model&#39;s task-step choices. - Evidence: src/agent.ts; src/harness/loop.ts; src/tools/index.ts. - Basis: direct source tracing and offline fake-provider/ACP executable smoke tests. - Confidence: high for the shipped decision route. - Caveats: no real remote model was called; observed execution is structural/mock-backed, not measured coding success under a live provider.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no shipped inter-S1 disturbance attenuation is established inside this one-session organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: possible conflicting edits or resource use among independent agents, but no first-party independent peer/child agent set is operated here. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none over relations among distinct S1 units. - Decision owner: the model sequences its own tool calls; the runtime serializes concurrent prompts on the same session. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: promptInFlight, tool-call ordering, ACP updates, local updateplan display, optional MCP connections. - Closure path: sequencing protects one session&#39;s history; there is no inter-S1 disturbance → coordination judgment → revised independent S1 behavior loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: plan publication and same-session serialization do not coordinate distinct autonomous operating units. - Evidence: src/agent.ts; src/tools/plan.ts; src/mcp/client.ts. - Basis: shipped runtime and available tool registry inspection. - Confidence: high for the declared first-party session boundary. - Caveats: an outside MCP server or multiple users could independently deploy agents, but that does not make their relations a shipped Sangxia S2 loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no whole-system current-control actor reallocating resources or commitments across a portfolio of S1 activity is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: current deadlines, partial tool failures, conversation length and user-approval constraints are handled operationally, without organizational-level current steering. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no actor owns a distinct cross-operation allocation, priority intervention or accountability return for the declared recursion. - Decision owner: S1 model chooses its own steps; static caps and permission rules are configured by operator/runtime; client can cancel a session. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: iteration/token/timeouts, abort controller, ACP permission mode, tool plan status and hook command results. - Closure path: operational attempts continue/stop under gates; no separate whole-organization view → current-control judgment → redirected S1 commitments loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: neither a fixed time budget nor a generic stop/approval UI establishes a VSM S3 decision owner; user permission is per action, not portfolio governance. - Evidence: src/agent.ts; src/harness/loop.ts; src/harness/permissions.ts. - Basis: inspected available resource/permission/current-state decision paths. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: operator could supervise a real-world organization with external tooling, but no qualifying parent-governed S3 mode is closed by this runtime itself.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no separate complementary audit path checks S1 claims with a distinguishable owner and returned findings. - Disturbance / variety regulated: undetected tool errors, unsafe actions and defective edits could benefit from audit, but the shipped hooks are general user-defined instrumentation/gates. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party independent or complementary auditor decides the validity of S1 reports. - Decision owner: external user-supplied hook scripts may deny or annotate calls; Sangxia executes and reports them without supplying a separate audit role/evidence path. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: pretooluse, posttooluse, lifecycle hooks, exit-code/error policies, command invocation and context injection. - Closure path: hooks may block a tool or feed diagnostics into the same model loop; there is no first-party separate claims-audit → finding → mandated correction path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a configurable program hook is a usable sensor or gate, not proof that an auditor actor, complementary access and sustained review right are shipped. - Evidence: src/hooks/index.ts; src/hooks/types.ts; src/harness/loop.ts. - Basis: inspected hook registry and tool integration. - Confidence: medium-high for stock/optional shipped configurations. - Caveats: an operator could write a reviewer as a hook, but that unobserved external composition cannot be credited as first-party S3.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: inspect the project environment and autonomously synthesize durable project guidance for future coding-agent operation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: missing/stale project-specific knowledge about architecture, conventions, commands and decisions that would otherwise make later coding sessions start without appropriate context. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose which inspected repository facts, working rules, architectural distinctions, decisions and TODOs become future project instruction/memory content. - Decision owner: the configured model actor running the dedicated project-initialization subturn; human only grants/skips initialization and may choose the scan boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: missing-file detection, explicit ACP operator permission request, scoped init prompt, read/list/glob/grep/write tool restriction, 12-iteration cap, durable AGENTS.md and .sangxia/memory.md, and systemPrompt() reload of project instructions. - Closure path: missing project instructions at the start of work → operator enables initialization → model inspects README/source/config and develops specific long-lived guidance → writes project memory files → live system prompt rebuild → later/new sessions load that guidance and change their coding context. - Boundary reachability: SangxiaAgent.prompt() invokes maybeInitializeProject before the requested work on the standard ACP/TUI route; its model-directed runTurn produces durable project files, then systemPrompt loads them from the same workspace. - External distinction: actual user project source, documentation, config, directory layout and optionally permitted parent-directory context outside the Sangxia runtime. - Future / prospective distinction: persisted instructions are explicitly designated as long-term project memory for later tasks and loaded at session start, rather than produced merely as an immediate answer. - Adaptation option generated: the model selects and writes rules/technical-stack conventions to AGENTS.md plus background/status/decisions/TODO knowledge to .sangxia/memory.md after examining the project. - Path back into current capability / S3: the initializer replaces the session system-prompt content via systemPrompt(session, initRoot) and persists it; newSession and relevant reload paths take project memory into coding-agent context, changing later operating guidance. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the parent approves initiation and scope, but neither static initialization prompt nor file writer determines project-specific judgments. The model owns their synthesis and the files alter subsequent agent context without a second parent adoption decision. An independent parent-governed adaptation mode is not established from a simple yes/no initiation gate. - Evidence: src/agent.ts; src/project-memory.ts; src/tools/fs-tools.ts. - Basis: traced shipped first-use initializer, model/tool capabilities, subsequent in-memory prompt rebuild and durable read path; fake-provider smoke evidence covers neighboring runtime behavior, not real model guidance quality. - Confidence: medium-high for ownership/reachability; effect on downstream success is unmeasured. - Caveats: bounded to user-consented, first-time missing-file initialization; not evidence of continuous external scanning, self-redesign or measured strategic success. Session restoration retains its saved system message except session-start hook additions, so the strongest guaranteed later-read path is a new session; do not infer live re-scan on every resumed session.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no ultimate purpose/identity policy loop governing the assistant as an organization is evidenced. - Disturbance / variety regulated: credentials, tool access and operator risk tolerance are handled as authentication or local effect permissions rather than ultimate institutional policy disputes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party S5 authority adjudicates competing mission/purpose/identity claims and returns binding policy changes. - Decision owner: human chooses access mode, configures provider and accepts/rejects tool and initialization prompts; maintainers own upstream source externally. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ACP authenticate, confirm/auto modes, per-tool remembered allowances, hook deny/ask behavior and system instructions. - Closure path: tactical permission and authentication gates affect a call/session, but no ultimate-policy issue → authorized identity decision → durable operational return is formed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a token check, user approval or stable system prompt is not by itself the final identity/policy governing function. - Evidence: src/agent.ts; src/harness/permissions.ts; src/config.ts. - Basis: traced authentication, configuration and policy/permission decision paths. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a larger human organization may have its own S5 outside this installed-agent boundary; repository maintainer activity is not attributed to a running session.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/2dd14c4373db9f46c72ccef31e6383c5840ca102/assessments/sa/sangxia-ai.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 2dd14c4373db9f46c72ccef31e6383c5840ca102\">2dd14c4 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-23T19:31:08+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/KNIGHT15235/likecc\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">LikeCC</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-23\">2026-10-23</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/KNIGHT15235/likecc/tree/4a9b77d281646385bf48fd85b8a03c4d28bbee23\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 4a9b77d281646385bf48fd85b8a03c4d28bbee23\">4a9b77d &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously implement and verify user-requested local coding tasks, including independently model-deciding child workers. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing code state, build/test failures, shell output, incomplete task requirements, permission denial and results of delegated coding. - Decisive decision or feedback right: model chooses file/shell operations and child task instructions, then selects subsequent actions on observed results. - Decision owner: LikeCC primary model and separately instantiated child/teammate model agents within their work scopes. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: provider, conversation loop, tool registry, permission checker, AgentTool, TaskManager, team/worktree setup and notifications. - Closure path: request → model tool/agent decision → checked native execution → result, error or child completion fed into a later model turn → revised action or final answer. - Boundary reachability: interactive app.py and headless main.py both instantiate the runtime plus the first-party AgentTool and Team system; separately enabled workers run through real model loops. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic code executes and restricts commands, but the substantive task choice and response to tool evidence are model decisions. - Evidence: agent.py, agenttool.py, main.py. - Basis: pinned first-party source and native script/unit tests; no provider-connected runs. - Confidence: high for installation and control wiring. - Caveats: passing deterministic tests does not establish LLM coding efficacy or quality of model decisions.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no complete first-party disturbance-specific inter-S1 coordination and correction circuit is established at the team recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: distinct coding teammates may propose incompatible edits, overlap shared task dependencies or require conflict resolution at integration. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no modeled, observable agent-owned assessment of an actual incompatible peer change followed by a conflict-resolving inter-S1 direction is established. - Decision owner: each worker selects its own coding tasks; team worktree construction is automatic and current lead dispatch is task routing rather than proved interference-specific arbitration. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: mandatory teammate-specific Git worktrees, shared task blocking fields, team mailbox and explicit coordinator prompt advice to serialize file-overlapping work. - Closure path: isolated teammate work and returned status exist, but there is no evidenced peer conflict/incompatibility detection → model-selected attenuation or integration decision → changed S1 execution based on that assessment. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: per-worker isolation and generic assignments prevent some direct overwrites; their creation is predetermined machinery rather than an agent deciding how to resolve detected inter-worker interference and closing that decision. - Evidence: agenttool.py, sharedtask.py, coordinator.py. - Basis: pinned source-level evaluation against five-witness S2 calibration. - Confidence: medium-high; conservative classification. - Caveats: a supported custom workflow could request a conflict review, but not enough is installed as S2-specific standard ownership here.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: C - Function: a dedicated first-party coordinator-mode constructor targets present whole-team work and priorities, but the full autonomous intervention loop is not wired. - Disturbance / variety regulated: simultaneously executing workers, changed readiness, task failures, unknown current worker progress and conflicting commitments within one local coding team. - Decisive decision or feedback right: coordinator model can synthesize results, issue initial/nested Agent assignments and choose next work from returned task notifications; the advertised follow-up/stop and whole-task inspection tools are missing from its live registry. - Decision owner: configured team-lead model selects initial and follow-on work, but the decisive whole-current feedback/reassignment right is only partially constructed. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: two-flag coordinator opt-in, first-party role prompt, TeamCreateTool, AgentTool, filtered lead registry, persisted TeamManager and child completion notifications. - Closure path: enabled lead → TeamCreate switches role → model delegates workers → runtime delivers completion or mailbox notifications → model may create new work; missing SendMessage/TaskStop registration and lead-side TaskList prevent the claimed direct current-worker correction/readback path. - Boundary reachability: optional configuration and environment opt-in activate the installed role when a team is created in the interactive runtime; headless likewise constructs TeamCreateTool, while all missing-tool limitations persist. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: coordinator-specific model discretion and role are purpose-built and first-party (hence C rather than generic delegation), but role text naming a tool cannot establish that tool&#39;s actual decision/feedback authority. - Whole-system current view: model sees its delegated task history and returned notifications but no registered live team-wide TaskList/member-status query; this incompleteness limits whole-current control. - Current-control decision scope: initial decomposition, dispatch and future new worker instructions are reachable; targeted follow-up, stop or reassignment of an active teammate is not fully available to the coordinator lead. - Evidence: coordinator.py, toolfilter.py, teamcreate.py, app.py, main.py. - Basis: positive function-specific optional constructor, with missing registration verified through both entry points and default registry. - Confidence: medium-high for constructor and negative actual-tool wiring conclusion. - Caveats: C does not credit closed autonomous current control. A future code change registering functional lead-side status/message/stop tools would require new pinned evidence and reassessment.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: in explicitly configured first-party Verification mode, independently challenge another worker&#39;s implementation/verification claim by inspecting actual project artifacts and test commands. - Disturbance / variety regulated: premature completion claims, self-serving implementation reports, missed regressions, unexecuted builds/tests and bugs hidden by producer assumptions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: a fresh specialized Verification model independently selects evidence checks and issues VERDICT: PASS / FAIL / PARTIAL; configured lead may use failed evidence to assign a new correcting worker. - Decision owner: different worker model with a new conversation and verification-specific instructions, not the implementation worker&#39;s own summary or test string. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: bundled verification.md, opt-in AgentLoader, per-agent filtered tool registry denying named file-edit tools, AgentTool background completion, first-party coordinator&#39;s fresh-verifier and error-follow-up policy. - Closure path: producing worker performs changes and claims completion → coordinator spawns separate freshly instructed Verification worker with access to actual project artifacts → verifier runs build/test/lint, independently challenges claim and returns explicit verdict via task notification → coordinator can assign correction or withhold verified completion. - Boundary reachability: set enableverificationagent: true so standard loader exposes the shipped named role; enable coordinator with config plus environment and create a team to install the mandate. For same-root ordinary subagents, the verifier can inspect the edited workspace; worktree-isolated teammates require explicit artifact/branch access or integration before the audit can see their edits. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the verifier, not a fixed CI assertion, makes a claim-relative judgment after independent artifact access; correction selection remains with the coordinating model. It is not an automatic runtime-enforced pass/fail gate. - Claim being audited: the implementation worker&#39;s assertion that the requested code change is complete, builds correctly and introduces no unreported regression. - Ordinary reporting path: implementer&#39;s own completion text, tool output and task-notification to the lead. - Complementary access path: fresh verifier reads implementation/configuration from disk and runs independent build, tests, type/lint and regression checks using its own tool calls, then reports actual commands/output and verdict. - Independence boundary: distinct fresh worker context and artifact access, restricted named edit tools; shared parent provider, potential shell side effects and absence of a runtime mandatory audit gate limit procedural independence. - Who acts on findings: lead model may launch a correcting worker or report incomplete verification; no downstream merge veto is mechanically guaranteed. - Evidence: verification.md, loader.py, agenttool.py, coordinator.py. - Basis: first-party shipped opt-in operating path, built-in specialist definition, native role/configuration and coordinator tests; no live verifier was run. - Confidence: medium; supported role/return path confirmed, actual quality and model compliance unknown. - Caveats: default setup leaves Verification disabled, the model must obey its prompt, and the independent worker must reach the actual modified artifact rather than an untouched sibling worktree. This is supported option capability, not proof that every LikeCC task was independently audited.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no installed outside/future environmental sensing and selected, retained organizational capability change is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing requirements and historical context affect coding sessions, but no future-oriented capability mismatch/adaptation loop is installed. - Decisive decision or feedback right: model can plan immediate tasks and user can configure roles/providers; no S4 owner decides durable capability redesign using an external future horizon. - Decision owner: absent for S4; task execution and plugin configuration belong to other recursions/functions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: project instructions, memory/session compaction, configurable prompts, user-defined agents, model configuration and Git history. - Closure path: retrieved context can alter current coding instructions, not a horizon scan → prospective option → adopted reusable capability → returned current-system support. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: implementing software for a user&#39;s present task is not necessarily strategic adaptation of the harness&#39;s own capabilities. - Evidence: prompts.py, config.py, agent.py. - Basis: local first-party source and runtime modes. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: downstream agent-authored plugins could be developed, but generic ability to write code does not itself establish the organizational S4 circuit.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no identity-defining legitimate policy arbitrator mediating present vs future organizational choices is installed. - Disturbance / variety regulated: dangerous tool effects and user permission boundaries are immediate operational constraints, not ultimate identity/purpose disputes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: operator/config sets policy and runtime enforces per-tool constraints; no S5 organization-wide ultimate-policy decision actor is specified. - Decision owner: no first-party S5 actor identified in the declared run-time recursion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: PermissionChecker, sandbox rules, bounded child permission inheritance, team role scope and configuration. - Closure path: immediate permission allow/deny returns to tool execution; no identity-level dispute → legitimate authority choice → durable organization-wide governing return. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: an operator&#39;s execution consent or static rule does not by itself constitute organizational purpose/identity governance. - Evidence: permissions/checker.py, main.py, config.py. - Basis: core permissions/configuration and local-vs-OSS boundary. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: legitimate human ownership of a real OSS organization is outside the analyzed local harness runtime.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/405f47ac27e8faaed531d00c91f6cfff3b3c66d9/assessments/li/likecc.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 405f47ac27e8faaed531d00c91f6cfff3b3c66d9\">405f47a &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-12T16:09:53+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/zai-org/ZCode\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ZCode</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-12\">2026-10-12</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/zai-org/ZCode/tree/872ad960de7ec172591f7e1952f7849229f94521\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 872ad960de7ec172591f7e1952f7849229f94521\">872ad96 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Direct first-party model-led coding and tool execution in the Agent CLI runtime. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Dynamic coding objectives, file/workspace changes, model responses and tool errors. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose tool calls and continue/repair from the returned tool observations. - Decision owner: Configured model-driven ZCode primary Agent running under the shipped CLI service. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Turn-loop/model-step, executor, permission broker, persisted session history. - Closure path: Task → model/tool-call turn → executable tool response → next model decision or completion. - Boundary reachability: The cited code is reached from the bundled first-party apps/zcode-cli runtime, whose CLI also supplies Desktop/Web hosting. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Model-facing tool choices are operational rather than deterministic UI events. - Evidence: apps/zcode-cli/packages/core/src/runtime/methods/turn-loop.ts; apps/zcode-cli/packages/core/src/runtime/methods/turn-model-step.ts; apps/zcode-cli/packages/core/src/runtime/methods/turn-tools.ts; README.en.md - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: ? - Function: Candidate inter-worker coordination; no source-grounded, interference-specific arbitration loop established. - Distinct S1 units: Main and separately running model/tool child agent sessions can operate concurrently. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Potential changes to overlapping files, conflicting work results and child request contention. - Attenuating coordination relation: No proven policy that detects a concrete inter-child conflict and alters their operational scope or placement. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Task status/notifications feed the parent, but conflict-specific corrective worker action remains unproven. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: S2 remains unknown, without treating ordinary task messages or technical scheduling as substantive S2. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Potential workspace resource contention between concurrently spawned agents. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Unresolved beyond configured tool availability, task registry and permissions. - Decision owner: Unresolved: service and parent may impose task control, not proven inter-S1 conflict arbitration. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Subagent child isolation configuration, task registry, parent messages and permission checks. - Closure path: Separate child results and message handling exist without verified conflict → attenuation → repair for competing workers. - Boundary reachability: The cited code is reached from the bundled first-party apps/zcode-cli runtime, whose CLI also supplies Desktop/Web hosting. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Session/task registration, generic delegation and permission checking do not establish S2. - Evidence: apps/zcode-cli/packages/core/src/subagent/runner.ts; apps/zcode-cli/packages/core/src/subagent/runtime-task-registry.ts; apps/zcode-cli/packages/core/src/runtime/methods/subagent.ts; apps/zcode-cli/packages/core/src/subagent/tool-policy.ts - Basis: unknown -\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: Model-led parent regulates ongoing independently running child agent tasks at the current team recursion. - Whole-system current view: Root has task IDs, child status/results and notifications for the children it launched. - Current-control decision scope: Child task allocation, interruption, follow-up and result integration within the bounded parent-child task set. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unfinished subordinate coding tasks, timeouts, interrupted child runs and new results. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Launch subordinate tasks, steer running child requests, wait/stop, integrate reports and select new work. - Decision owner: Primary ZCode model-guided coordinator session through shipped SubagentPort. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Child runner, SubagentPort, task snapshots, coordinator response and steering interface. - Closure path: Child status/result → parent model sees tool response or asynchronous notification → follow-up/steering or next child task. - Boundary reachability: The cited code is reached from the bundled first-party apps/zcode-cli runtime, whose CLI also supplies Desktop/Web hosting. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Decisive model task allocation and follow-up can alter active worker runs; task registry merely implements control. - Evidence: apps/zcode-cli/packages/core/src/subagent/runner.ts; apps/zcode-cli/packages/core/src/runtime/methods/subagent.ts; apps/zcode-cli/packages/core/src/subagent/message-steering.ts; apps/zcode-cli/packages/core/src/subagent/runtime-task-registry.ts - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: ? - Function: Separate operational audit path not established even though completion verification helpers and review surfaces exist. - Claim being audited: Child or root agent delivered the requested code/tasks correctly. - Ordinary reporting path: Child completion and model/tool outputs return to root via task runner and notifications. - Complementary access path: Unproven source/artifact sampling by a separate reviewer; completion helper can inspect claims without a second operating audit path. - Independence boundary: No guaranteed materially independent reviewer decision owner established. - Who acts on findings: Root continuation may use checks, but returned independent audit findings and corrective closure remain unproven. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Incorrect code/task completion claims or missing verification evidence. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Possible target-completion checking, but independent source sampling and corrective return are not established. - Decision owner: Unresolved separate reviewer authority; no independent audit actor guaranteed by core runtime. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Target completion verification helper and standard tool feedback. - Closure path: Model output/checks may feed same agent, but a materially independent challenger with primary artifact access is not proven. - Boundary reachability: The cited code is reached from the bundled first-party apps/zcode-cli runtime, whose CLI also supplies Desktop/Web hosting. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Single-agent tests and helper names alone are insufficient for S3 semantics. - Evidence: apps/zcode-cli/packages/core/src/runtime/methods/target-completion-verification.ts; apps/zcode-cli/packages/core/src/subagent/runner.ts; apps/zcode-cli/packages/core/src/runtime/methods/turn-loop.ts - Basis: unknown -\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: ? - Function: Retained project or subagent memories and dynamic workflows may influence later tasks without a proved outside-and-then capability evolution loop. - External distinction: Provider/context and project changes can enter through tools or memory, not a demonstrated strategic environmental-scan unit. - Future / prospective distinction: Later recall and project memory are temporally future-facing but not sufficient organizational anticipation. - Adaptation option generated: No definite capability-changing option/owner documented in the reviewed running mode. - Path back into current capability / S3: Prompt/session reuse may affect later actions, without proving S4-to-S3 redesign closure. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing environments, new project requirements and capability gaps. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Not established which model-led agent chooses future capability adaptation based on external horizon scanning. - Decision owner: Unresolved among task agent, configured workflows, app owner and memory subsystem. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Persistent subagent memory, project-context and workflow facilities. - Closure path: Stored experience → later prompt/context, but no traced external prospective option → adoption into current operational capability. - Boundary reachability: The cited code is reached from the bundled first-party apps/zcode-cli runtime, whose CLI also supplies Desktop/Web hosting. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Persistent memory or model summaries do not automatically establish S4. - Evidence: apps/zcode-cli/packages/core/src/subagent/persistent-memory.ts; apps/zcode-cli/packages/core/src/agent/message-history.ts; apps/zcode-cli/packages/core/src/runtime/methods/subagent.ts - Basis: unknown -\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: ? - Function: First-party permission and configuration governance exists but not a complete ultimate identity/policy loop. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: Which coding-agent purpose, permissible authority and final governance applies at root/team level. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: Human/user settings and deterministic policies govern tools; no qualifying complete model/parent S5 closure proven. - Return-to-operation path: Permissions change individual tool admit/deny, not proven ultimate organizational policy adaptation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Trust/permission boundaries, operator confirmations and tool access to host environment. - Decisive decision or feedback right: User/developer permissions and policies may constrain actions; ultimate-policy decision and organization-level return unproven. - Decision owner: Human and deterministic permission engine; a qualifying agent/parent S5 is not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Permission broker, subagent tool allowlist, session capabilities and workspace restrictions. - Closure path: Permission allow/deny → local tool execution; no proved ultimate organizational identity/policy rewrite and feedback. - Boundary reachability: The cited code is reached from the bundled first-party apps/zcode-cli runtime, whose CLI also supplies Desktop/Web hosting. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Safety gates or provider permission modes do not establish S5. - Evidence: apps/zcode-cli/packages/core/src/subagent/tool-policy.ts; apps/zcode-cli/packages/core/src/runtime/methods/subagent.ts; apps/zcode-cli/packages/core/src/runtime/methods/turn-tools.ts - Basis: unknown -\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/990458bae057708366e1e0def9d3c2afbc23e813/assessments/zcode.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 990458bae057708366e1e0def9d3c2afbc23e813\">990458b &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-03T18:39:20+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/mindweave-cli/mindweave\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Mindweave</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-03\">2026-10-03</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/mindweave-cli/mindweave/tree/7a108f043dbfca273dd420d193803f41040a64b7\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 7a108f043dbfca273dd420d193803f41040a64b7\">7a108f0 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform environment-facing software work by inspecting repository state, choosing tools, editing code, executing commands/tests and reacting to returned evidence. - Disturbance / variety regulated: arbitrary codebase structure, failing checks, diagnostics, command output, provider variation, tool errors, user feedback and implementation alternatives. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next substantive coding/search/command action, interpret returned results, revise the approach, delegate bounded work and decide when to report. - Decision owner: the active model-backed Mindweave actor and, within assigned scope, each child model-backed subagent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: provider-neutral engine; tool registry; deterministic guards; project memory/governor injection; file/shell/MCP tools; session persistence; language-server diagnostics; budgets and cancellation. - Closure path: user/project state → model request → selected tool/action → runtime executes/refuses → result returns into the transcript → later model turn changes subsequent work. - Boundary reachability: this is the installed terminal agent&#39;s normal runtime; spawnsubagent reuses the same first-party engine for bounded children. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model actor leaves deterministic tools and guards but removes the open-ended judgment that chooses and interprets coding actions. - Evidence: README.md; src/dynamo/engine.ts; src/tools/subagent.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: external provider inference is a dependency; the credited role/tool/feedback loop is first-party.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: prevent destructive interference between concurrently delegated S1 units by allowing parallel read-only work while serializing mutating child agents. - Disturbance / variety regulated: two child coding agents editing the same project concurrently can race, overwrite changes or reason from incompatible intermediate filesystem states. - Decisive decision or feedback right: classify a delegated worker as read-only versus mutating and apply the corresponding parallel-versus-serial execution lane. - Decision owner: constructor/runtime path. The parent model supplies readonly, but Mindweave&#39;s first-party scheduler owns the hard rule that only read-only workers are concurrency-safe and mutating workers run alone. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: process-wide semaphore; per-tool isConcurrencySafe; child-session forking; one-level recursion cap; step budgets. - Closure path: multiple delegated children are requested → runtime inspects the child mode → read-only workers may execute concurrently while a mutating worker is serialized → subsequent child filesystem actions cannot race with another editing child → results return to the parent. - Boundary reachability: this policy is implemented directly in shipped spawnsubagent and used by the standard engine&#39;s concurrent tool execution. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the collision attenuation is guaranteed by constructor/runtime scheduling rather than an autonomous actor negotiating between workers; the first-party S2-specific path is nevertheless complete enough to meet the constructor threshold. - Evidence: src/tools/subagent.ts; src/dynamo/engine.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: generic delegation and the semaphore capacity ceiling are not the witness; the positive mapping is the explicit edit-race prevention rule. - Distinct S1 units: multiple forked Mindweave child agents, each running the same model/tool engine on a bounded project task. - Inter-S1 disturbance: concurrent mutating children can race on shared project files and invalidate each other&#39;s working state. - Attenuating coordination relation: runtime admits parallelism only for read-only children and routes mutating children through a serial execution lane. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: the scheduling classification determines whether a child may begin concurrently and therefore what filesystem state its later tool calls can observe/mutate. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the implementation explicitly names concurrent edit races as the disturbance and changes execution concurrency specifically to eliminate that interference.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Function: regulate the mission&#39;s current commitments through a user-approved whole plan that binds subsequent execution and returns failed-plan decisions to the user rather than allowing silent replanning. - Disturbance / variety regulated: an autonomous coding actor can drift from an agreed implementation plan, expand scope, or improvise around a failing current step in ways that change the user&#39;s active commitments without authority. - Decisive decision or feedback right: approve the full plan that governs the work and, when execution evidence shows the approved path is no longer viable, decide whether/how to replan. - Decision owner: the user/operator as legitimate parent authority in the supported plan mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: read-only plan mode; exitplan; persisted .mindweave/plan.md; standing-knowledge injection; repeat-failure/divergence detector; automatic clearing/completion of the plan artifact after the governed work turn. - Closure path: model researches and proposes a full plan → user approves → runtime persists exact approved plan and injects it into execution → model must follow the approved commitments → repeated plan-step failure triggers a hard stop with instruction not to improvise → user receives the unresolved current-control choice and can authorize a new plan. - Boundary reachability: plan mode and plan approval are standard interactive Mindweave features; the approved artifact is read by the same runtime that executes the work, including across sessions. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model proposes and executes, but the authority to establish or replace the whole approved commitment set belongs to the user in this mode. - Evidence: src/dynamo/engine.ts; src/dynamo/planArtifact.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: ordinary user prompts/approvals are not credited. The witness is the distinct plan mode with whole-plan authority, persisted returned commitment and fail-closed replan path. - Whole-system current view: the complete approved implementation plan is the mission-level set of current commitments presented to the parent before execution; the runtime retains that exact plan as standing knowledge while work proceeds. - Current-control decision scope: establish/replace the mission&#39;s current implementation commitments and decide what to do when the active approved plan cannot be followed. - Parent mode: user approval creates the active plan; repeated execution failure returns the decision to that same parent authority; the returned plan governs later operation. - No autonomous/constructor base mode is claimed: todo/re-scope/verification guards support current execution, but this review does not treat those fixed backstops or model-authored task lists as an independently established S3 ownership mode.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge completed code/work through a separate adversarial verifier agent before its verdict is relied on. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a worker/main coding actor can produce plausible but incorrect changes, skip meaningful checks or self-report success without evidence. - Decisive decision or feedback right: judge the already-produced work as pass, fail or partial after independently executing checks and attempting to break it. - Decision owner: a separate model-backed verifier child created with verify:true and verifier-specific instructions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: read-only child tool restriction; separate child session/context; verifier system prompt; required command/output report shape; deterministic evidence counter that downgrades an unevidenced pass. - Closure path: parent identifies work to audit → launches adversarial verifier with original request/change scope → verifier independently reads/runs checks without edit authority → verifier returns evidence-backed verdict → parser may downgrade unsupported pass → parent receives verdict/concerns and can fix, continue or accept. - Boundary reachability: verifier mode is a documented option of the shipped spawnsubagent tool, not a test-only evaluator. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the substantive adversarial test selection and pass/fail judgment are made by a separate model actor; deterministic parsing only enforces evidence hygiene. - Evidence: src/tools/subagent.ts; src/tools/verifyReport.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the ordinary deterministic VERIFYNUDGE is not the S3 witness; it only detects “edited but no check”. The positive mapping relies on the separate adversarial child. - Claim being audited: that already-performed code changes satisfy the original request and survive meaningful execution/boundary checks. - Ordinary reporting path: main/worker coding actor edits and reports its result. - Complementary access path: verifier child is forked in a separate session with a dedicated adversarial prompt and read-only tool set, then runs its own checks against project reality. - Independence boundary: the verifier cannot edit the project, does not share the parent&#39;s conversational reasoning and must produce command/output evidence; an unevidenced pass is mechanically rejected as partial. - Who acts on findings: the parent Mindweave actor receives the verdict and concerns and can take corrective operational action.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party prospective adaptation loop was established at the selected mission recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established at S4 level. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. Cross-session memory, project notes, web/MCP access and session summaries preserve useful information, but no shipped S4 actor was found that develops adaptation options from future/external distinctions and changes persistent organizational capability/strategy. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: savememory; automatic memory index; MINDWEAVE.md; project/directory notes; web/MCP tools; session memory/compaction; provider/model switching. - Closure path: not applicable; no distinct external-and-prospective intelligence → adaptation option → current capability/S3 return loop was established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the operational actor can remember facts and use them later, but durable context is not by itself prospective organizational adaptation. - Evidence: src/memory/autoMemory.ts; src/memory/projectNotes.ts; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: learning/persistence may improve later S1 decisions; that effect does not satisfy the stronger S4 functional/closure threshold.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no distinct first-party identity/ultimate-policy issue-resolution loop was established at the selected mission recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established at S5 level. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established as an S5 right. The user can state durable governor rules/forbidden paths/commands and the runtime enforces them, but these are general standing constraints rather than evidence of a distinct identity/ultimate-policy issue reaching a legitimate S5 authority. - Decision owner: not established for an S5 function. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: governor rule files; forbid/unforbid tools; live governance reload; mechanical path/command/MCP enforcement; project notes and skills. - Closure path: no qualifying identity/ultimate-policy issue → authoritative decision → returned governance loop was established. User rule requests do return into operation, but the reviewed examples/path are ordinary project execution constraints. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: governor comments explicitly say the user decides what to make a rule/forbid and tools only record/enforce it; enforcement authority does not itself establish S5. - Evidence: src/tools/governorTools.ts; src/dynamo/engine.ts; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a deployment that uses the governor for genuine mission identity/ultimate-policy decisions could motivate a different recursion-specific analysis; generic standing rules are not enough here.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/7c3e890b8e2bb1c88e87c4c4988e1cbba3aaf50e/assessments/mindweave.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 7c3e890b8e2bb1c88e87c4c4988e1cbba3aaf50e\">7c3e890 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-23T18:58:06+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/KarmSakha/karmx-agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">karmX</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-23\">2026-10-23</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/KarmSakha/karmx-agent/tree/34a1f940e185db7dbfedba87be8206705e79b89d\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 34a1f940e185db7dbfedba87be8206705e79b89d\">34a1f94 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously execute repository-development tasks through a provider/model/tool loop, with optional independently deciding subagent and top-level worker sessions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: project contents, shell errors, tool denials, web UI state, prompt uncertainty, model feedback and asynchronous work results. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the LLM chooses which tool calls and arguments to issue and how to adapt after returned observations; separate delegate sessions follow their own model loops. - Decision owner: model-backed main karmX agent and spawned model-backed delegates/top-level sessions in supported modes. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: provider adapter, tool calls, Rust execution, permission modes, shell/browser/context platform extensions, persistent conversations, compaction and summon task metadata. - Closure path: incoming development request → provider chooses tools/sidekick tasks → runtime executes and returns result or denial → model chooses subsequent code changes, checks or user response. - Boundary reachability: compiled CLI connects its agent/runtime and enabled platform-extension registry; browser/context and summon entries are first-party default-enabled, while orchestrator can additionally create top-level S1s in an explicitly enabled mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model selects task-specific repository changes and revises them from tool feedback; deterministic execution and permissions only constrain those choices. - Evidence: agent.rs, statemachine/opstoolcalling.rs, summon.rs. - Basis: explicit operating docs and frozen source; no live LLM run. - Confidence: high for architecture and reachable decision path. - Caveats: no model correctness or practical coding quality is inferred from source and deterministic tests.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: distinct model-backed delegates can coexist, but no complete first-party interference-specific coordination loop is evidenced for their concurrent repository writes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: sidekick/delegates sharing files can edit the same path and conflict; the source explicitly warns this can happen. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the delegate tool advises the main model to partition files, but neither tracks actual file ownership nor mediates overlap or returns a collision-specific coordination response. - Decision owner: parent model may choose task decomposition in normal S1 work; no independently established S2 ownership closure. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: delegate/sidekick, load, asynchronous task handles, separate model contexts and tool-description advice to avoid same-file work. - Closure path: child summaries return to the parent for task synthesis, but no first-party inter-S1 conflict detection/negotiation/reservation → decision → adapted sibling operation loop is exposed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the advisory sentence can influence a model, but the checked delegate path neither carries resolved per-child file scopes nor traces conflict-specific feedback that changes other child operations. Top-level orchestrator messaging is generic transport absent a particular interference-regulation use. - Evidence: summon.rs, localfusion/mod.rs, orchestrator.rs. - Basis: source explicitly describes the collision risk and advisory-only response. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: users can voluntarily partition tasks or use separate worktrees, but no sufficient first-party specific closure is evidenced in this frozen implementation.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: coordinate current commitments and execution capacity over several independently operating top-level agent sessions in an explicitly enabled first-party orchestrator mode. - Disturbance / variety regulated: disparate session progress, idle/busy workers, current work commitments, agent capacity and interruption/reassignment needs. - Whole-system current view: orchestrator.listsessions surfaces session types, status, latest updates and message counts, and viewsession exposes current conversation evidence or a summary; the session manager spans the local current agent cohort. - Current-control decision scope: top-level orchestrating model chooses which new session to start, what message/assignment to send to an existing session and when to interrupt a busy one. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the model sees current agent roster/status and decides interventions over active peer work; not merely an automatic concurrency quota. - Decision owner: model-backed top-level agent in optional orchestrator-enabled mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: session manager, registered orchestrator tools, agent manager, start/interrupt permission checks, cancellation tokens, provider/session binding and response delivery. - Closure path: model inspects current roster/conversation → chooses start/message/interrupt tool → runtime creates or changes running sessions → subsequent operations follow new assignment or cancellation → model can reinspect current state. - Boundary reachability: platformextensions/mod.rs registers orchestrator as a first-party extension even though defaultenabled: false and hidden: true; normal platform extension configuration permits the supported opt-in mode. No external custom orchestrator actor or implementation is required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: without the model&#39;s discretionary choice about which session to affect, the runtime only lists/enforces requested actions; it cannot select current priorities/commitments on its own. The optional path is genuine autonomous current control, unlike default lead/sidekick delegation by itself. - Evidence: orchestrator.rs, platformextensions/mod.rs, config/extensions.rs. - Basis: structural, function-specific top-level cross-session control; the frozen Goose-derived first-party constructor is retained by this fork. - Confidence: high for advertised first-party optional wiring, with opt-in scope explicitly bounded. - Caveats: ordinary default CLI operation does not automatically activate this mode; S3=A records supported availability, not usage in every local run.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no established independent complementary audit judgment returns into whole-current control. - Disturbance / variety regulated: errors or inaccurate delegated coding reports can require review, but a separate first-party audit governance path is not wired. - Decisive decision or feedback right: lead guidance asks to inspect the sidekick&#39;s diff, yet the long SIDEKICKPROMPT audit-like template is not consumed by the operating agent; regular tests and browser previews are direct work verification. - Decision owner: no distinct independent S3 audit actor established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: file/diff tools, sidekick report contract, model tool results, user review of artifacts and unit/eval scripts. - Closure path: sidekick results or browser DOM observations return to the producing/lead S1 decision path; no independent complementary sampler → review finding → S3 corrective return. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: asking the same lead to read its worker&#39;s diff is not an evidentially independent complementary audit circuit, and unmounted template text cannot supply ownership. - Evidence: localfusion/mod.rs, summon.rs, orchestrator.rs. - Basis: source-level audit-role and reachable-path inspection. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a separately configured auditor could close this function downstream, but cannot be borrowed into the shipped runtime assessment.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no first-party forward-looking external environmental model driving durable organizational capability redesign is closed. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing code state, browsers and user prompts present current-work uncertainties rather than a monitored future strategic environment. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the model selects retrieval/enhancement/rerank for the present request, while human config chooses providers and extension use; no future-oriented adaptation option receives organizational approval/return. - Decision owner: no S4-specific prospective choice owner established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: context engine&#39;s workspace retrieval/relevance ranking, prompt enhancer, browser preview DOM/console, Chrome CDP control, predictive context compaction, user-maintained skills and provider configuration. - Closure path: context/browser observations can revise the current coding turn; they do not form an outside-and-then scan → adaptation option → changed reusable organizational capability → present-control loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: even model-backed prompt enhancement and browser inspection improve present task execution, not necessarily the future operating identity/capabilities of the harness. - Evidence: contextengine/mod.rs, browserpreview/mod.rs, computercontrol/mod.rs. - Basis: bounded built-in capabilities/source and documentation. - Confidence: high for lack of proven prospective capability closure in inspected modes. - Caveats: an operator can ask the coding agent to build a new capability, but this remains external task assignment rather than an S4-specific organizational adaptive function.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no legitimate ultimate-policy or organizational identity decision path is established in the operating multi-session agent. - Disturbance / variety regulated: per-tool safety/permissions and session authority matter, but are tactical task constraints rather than identity-level disputes between S3 current control and S4 adaptation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: model can issue task/tool requests, permission manager and human operator choose whether particular actions are allowed; code enforces session relation bounds. - Decision owner: no first-party S5 agent or legitimate parent identity-policy owner with returned organizational closure evidenced. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: GooseMode, permission manager, child role limits, working-directory checks, extension configuration, prompts and maintainers&#39; repository governance. - Closure path: tool approval or denial returns to the requesting coding turn; no ultimate identity/policy issue → legitimate authority decision → durable governing return loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model-visible permissions and provider configuration do not grant authority over the system&#39;s defining purpose or arbitrate S3/S4 tensions. - Evidence: agent.rs, summon.rs, orchestrator.rs. - Basis: bounded review of identity, permission and session authority paths. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: maintainers may make strategic choices for the OSS project, but their external release organization is excluded from this local runtime boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/97bfad8a82a406f852f274e4e5a56f8681ad651c/assessments/ka/karmx.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 97bfad8a82a406f852f274e4e5a56f8681ad651c\">97bfad8 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"2\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-17T19:38:49+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Gen-Verse/ScienceBuddy\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ScienceBuddy / Simple-SciBuddy</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>2</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-17\">2026-10-17</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Gen-Verse/ScienceBuddy/tree/53177995fd549068cbe25282273bed771e843f50\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 53177995fd549068cbe25282273bed771e843f50\">5317799 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: A scientific task model solves individual tasks through first-party program-directed generation, tool execution and submission cycles. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Varied scientific user questions, reasoning uncertainty, tool/answer format failures, changing observations and additional simulated researcher feedback. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The task model chooses reasoning/output and possible tool calls under a runnable Python program&#39;s control, responds to actual observations and submits a scientific answer. A replacement candidate can change harness call/order/context strategies. - Decision owner: First-party run(task, api) program plus connected model-inference runtime; the scientific task&#39;s model owns content decisions, and frozen code owns interaction/action feedback. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: API.generate/execute/submit, bounded broker messages, isolated controller sandbox, input/token limits, recorded trajectories and task/verifier datasets. - Closure path: scientific question → harness/model action → actual execution/tool output → model continuation and submitted answer → recorded result/reply, optionally next conversation turn. - Boundary reachability: coevolve.phase.batch calls harness.broker.runepisode with first-party scientific run(task,api); the broker executes the installed Python harness controller, mediates actual model generation/execution/submission and saves episode events. Same path feeds inner and validation tasks. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The task model and harness generate contingent actions and explanations from actual task/tool feedback, not a prewritten fixed answer table. - Evidence: src/simplescibuddy/harness/scientific.py; src/simplescibuddy/harness/broker.py; src/simplescibuddy/harness/controller.py; src/simplescibuddy/coevolve/phase.py. - Basis: Executable first-party task-agent/model-feedback path. - Confidence: High source path; no model-produced empirical result validated in this review. - Caveats: RL training and model weights are external dependencies; hosted scientific workspace is excluded.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No source-backed inter-S1 mutual disturbance or interference-regulation feedback established beyond shared batch throughput caps. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Different candidate and parent evaluations simultaneously compete for inference throughput, but code does not demonstrate a documented resource collision/oscillation remedied by adaptive S2 balancing. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A configured semaphore serializes/caps concurrent task batches; no separate decision adapts conflicting S1 operating mandates or co-activity after observing a disturbance. - Decision owner: Deterministic batch executor and configured worker concurrency limit. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: One shared validation semaphore across parent/candidate evaluations, task-specific isolated runtime and result recording. - Closure path: Task batch → semaphore admission → episode completion frees token. This is general capacity management, not a demonstrated conflict signal → inter-S1 correction → feedback to changed sibling operations. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Pure concurrency limiting without observed inter-operational conflict and responsive attenuation must not be inflated into autonomous or constructor-owned S2. - Evidence: src/simplescibuddy/coevolve/phase.py; src/simplescibuddy/coevolve/selection.py; src/simplescibuddy/harness/broker.py. - Basis: Negative bounded causal search of shared execution and parallel candidate evaluation. - Confidence: Medium; improvement would need documented clash between distinct S1 activity lanes and a meaningful active attenuation return. - Caveats: The semaphore is a real first-party concurrency guard; exclusion of S2 does not deny resource protection.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: C - Function: Regulate present multi-episode experiment phase and commitments: selected harness/model, Train/Val/Test boundaries, current candidate submissions/validation tasks, task execution budget, training handoff and fail-closed state. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unsupported Python candidate programs, incomplete or incomparable validation coverage, evidence leakage into proposals, failed workers, changed frozen data and unstable training/assessment. - Decisive decision or feedback right: First-party coevolve.loop.run schedules current harness learning and RL model updates, freezes dataset identities, advances Train cursor, calls candidate selector, triggers or halts training based on selected improvement and checks worker result before updating current model/harness. - Decision owner: Deterministic first-party coevolution/controller code and static experimental budget/config. The auxiliary model proposes and task model operates; neither owns whole-experiment now-control. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Frozen identities, Train cursor, manifests, complete heldout Val match, preflight, selection winner and validation trace, request/result integrity and stop conditions. - Closure path: Current episodes, candidate readiness and validation outcomes → source controller chooses reject/adopt/stop/train/new model → changes active harness/model and next execution commitments → writes persisted phase history and repeats. - Whole-system current view: Parent and candidate harness identities, current task-model checkpoint, frozen train/val/test source, worker status, phase counters and comparative evaluation results for the whole experiment. - Current-control decision scope: Govern current allocation to scientific task batches, compared candidate evaluations, training worker, later phase start and hard stop, not just an individual scientific task. - Boundary reachability: coevolve.loop.run invokes harnessevolve and reads phasesummary[&quot;improved&quot;] before publishing/claiming training requests; selectcandidates decides adoption from complete Val results, and validateresult supplies next model to the next harness phase. This is the runnable experiment entry, not an offline report. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Selection/admission/train handoff and return are explicit first-party rules, not an autonomous policy model choosing all current resource mandates; S3=C. - Evidence: src/simplescibuddy/coevolve/loop.py; src/simplescibuddy/coevolve/phase.py; src/simplescibuddy/coevolve/selection.py; src/simplescibuddy/coevolve/protocol.py. - Basis: Installed full-current experiment and verified phase transitions. - Confidence: High for deterministic current control, bounded to experiment recursion. - Caveats: No enterprise lab portfolio governance inferred; SkyRL provides computation inside a structured worker contract.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: Independently check proposed candidate claims of improved scientific task performance against privately retained ground-truth evidence, and return veto/admission to current control. - Disturbance / variety regulated: A model-proposed harness change can hallucinate improvement, accidentally overfit public examples, fail to submit valid answers or contaminate proposed evidence with Val/Test labels. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Host private-reference verifier grades actual completed Val episodes, requires complete same-task coverage and zero candidate execution errors; candidate replaces parent only on strictly higher correct-task count. No model critic owns that alternative correctness decision. - Decision owner: First-party deterministic held-out verifier and selection mechanism, with private reference answers withheld from the candidate/proposer; choice is constructor-owned. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Data verifier, frozen Val task IDs, task-answer collection from episodes, parent/candidate same-model comparison, valid program preflight, private ground-truth isolation. - Closure path: Model-improver proposed harness improvement → actual unseen held-out Val task rollout and host private-reference scoring → controller rejects/accepts replacement → selected/rejected harness changes future S1 operation and next evolution round. - Claim being audited: Whether a proposed Python harness program is scientifically valid operationally and actually improves scored task success relative to current parent. - Ordinary reporting path: Improver propose with hypothesis and public execution feedback; candidate program runs episodes and submits answers. - Complementary access path: Independent private reference labels and actual held-out Val execution paths are hidden from proposal inputs; structured host scoring verifies answers under matched tasks/model and full coverage. - Independence boundary: Model improver cannot read Val/Test oracle labels or score before proposals freeze; controller owns the verifier and may share code authorship but not the model&#39;s candidate claims. This is a constructor/host-scored audit, not a separate autonomous model judge. - Who acts on findings: selectcandidates computes exact parent/candidate accuracy and checks zero errors; chooses winner or retains parent. The selected source file and its validation receipt feed next harness/model phase through harnessevolve/coevolve.loop. - Boundary reachability: harnessevolve calls selectcandidates, which runs every candidate and parent on the Val panel and only writes status: applied for strict improvements; the loop uses selected harness file for later Train/RL phases. Verified on source, not inference speculation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A separate private oracle determines whether current change claims hold, but comparison/admission logic is deterministic, so S3=C rather than A. Optional auxiliary PRM feedback interpretation is hypothesis generation, not promoted as an independent auditor. - Evidence: src/simplescibuddy/coevolve/selection.py; src/simplescibuddy/data/verifier.py; src/simplescibuddy/coevolve/phase.py; src/simplescibuddy/coevolve/evidence.py. - Basis: First-party source-attested independent verifier and binding quality-admission path. - Confidence: Medium-high for held-out auditable comparison; this is not a human external independent review. - Caveats: Same dataset owner/controller may govern protocols; performance verification is not proof of every scientific intermediate step. Full Val coverage does not guarantee deployment generalization.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: Prospective improve the operational harness&#39;s future scientific-task capacity by diagnosing externally encountered task environment, proposing alternative complete executable harness designs, testing their future performance and returning an adopted change into actual ongoing S3/S1 control. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Poor scientific task success across diverse external Train scenarios, recurrent tool errors, context clipping, unreliable reasoning, shifting effectiveness of model/harness combinations after RL updates. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Independent auxiliary model Improver selects evidence-grounded hypotheses and full replacement Python harness programs; source controller tests alternatives on heldout Val and adopts only validated improvements, then uses selected program in next task-model and evolution stages. - Decision owner: First-party configured auxiliary model owns new harness idea/program content and selects prospective modifications; deterministic S3 selection holds performance/admission rights. No external hosted ScienceBuddy product decisions imported. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Public episode evidence and validated feedback, explicit program schema, bounded read/propose/skip, program AST safety gate, Train preflight, matched Val selection and actual file replacement, alternating GRPO model update. - Closure path: Task-environment episodes/feedback and tool failure → auxiliary-model future harness hypotheses/complete Python programs → independent Val testing → S3 select/adopt → actual new run(task, api) program guides future task episodes / model training → new model checkpoint returns for next adaptation phase. - External distinction: The external Train task distribution and observed researcher/tool errors or feedback vary beyond the currently executing task and create empirically distinct demands on the agent harness, from task episode evidence rather than maintainer memory. - Future / prospective distinction: Candidate changes target the performance of subsequent heldout Val and future Train/Test tasks after the present task episode; the next model/harness cycle is deliberately separate in time from the observed failures. - Adaptation option generated: The auxiliary model creates executable alternative complete Python run(task, api) harness programs that change prompt construction, tool orchestration, context management and submission handling; independent Val comparison selects or rejects each candidate. - Path back into current capability / S3: Host S3 selection writes the winner as the active harness file, uses it for subsequent episodes and RL model training, and feeds the exported model checkpoint plus harness to the next coevolution phase, closing the option-to-operation return. - External and prospective orientation: The future task distribution and observed researcher/tool environment provide performance pressures; the auxiliary model develops design alternatives to change the agent&#39;s operating harness for tasks beyond current episode, rather than only answering the current scientific question. - Adaptation options: Full Python run replacements modify prompt organization, parsing, tool use, context/memory and control flow; a candidate can be rejected with history or be adopted under strictly better Val accuracy. - Two-way return with current control: Current episode failures and validation feedback supply improver inputs; the S3 experiment controller accepts a verified candidate, replaces active harness, trains/updates model and schedules the next current task batch with the changed capability. Future cycle feedback re-enters improver. - Boundary reachability: coevolve.phase.harnessevolve gathers source-safe public evidence and calls coevolve.program.propose; selection.selectcandidates can select a different actual harness.py file; coevolve.loop.run receives returned harness and trains/starts subsequent rounds with it. This is executable first-party adaptation in configured evolution mode, not passive documentation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The auxiliary model autonomously authors new harness program/hypothesis options based on evidence; constructor selection supplies safety/performance gating. Thus adaptive design generation is A within strict model+host composite, not merely tool/config hot reload. - Evidence: src/simplescibuddy/coevolve/program.py; src/simplescibuddy/coevolve/evidence.py; src/simplescibuddy/coevolve/improver.py; src/simplescibuddy/coevolve/selection.py; src/simplescibuddy/coevolve/loop.py. - Basis: Exact first-party source of longitudinal harness proposals, empirical selection and return-to-operation path. - Confidence: High that mechanism is implemented; no observed percentage improvement or completed multi-round GPU training asserted. - Caveats: S4 applies to configured evolution mode, not every isolated task run. Model training stack and third-party CUDA packages are dependencies, and positive score is not a claim of scientific truth outside frozen tasks.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No installed ultimate ethos/identity/policy dispute resolution for the scientific agent organization; predefined scientific task and improvement goals are inherited rather than governed. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Public/private dataset confidentiality, artifact tampering, invalid program execution, training protocol mismatches and runtime permissions are handled by fixed rules. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Experiment controller enforces frozen task split, code safety/preflight, model identity checks and strict Val improvement; it does not decide whether maximizing a frozen verifier&#39;s task accuracy is the legitimate ultimate purpose or negotiate value conflicts with a parent institution. - Decision owner: Maintainer / human experiment designer defines science goal and policy/config; first-party loop executes and reports under those constraints. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Immutable protocol manifests, private oracle data, AST program validation, task-content redaction, reproducible Train cursor, checkpoint hashes and stop reasons. - Closure path: Fixed experiment rules → operational acceptance/rejection. No independent legitimate-policy conflict → highest-level purpose choice → binding return to S1–S4. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Self-improving harness code does not imply self-governing mission/ethos; neither static experiment config nor signed checkpoints supply policy S5. - Evidence: src/simplescibuddy/coevolve/loop.py; src/simplescibuddy/coevolve/protocol.py; src/simplescibuddy/coevolve/program.py; configs/defaults.json. - Basis: Bounded negative source assessment of top-level objective and legitimacy ownership. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: External maintainers or research institutions may be ultimate-policy owners beyond this open-source runtime; not automatically scored as parent S5.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/6ee3760070fd875c36798fef7b94cd326f1e4789/assessments/sciencebuddy-simple-scibuddy.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 6ee3760070fd875c36798fef7b94cd326f1e4789\">6ee3760 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-05T21:34:22+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/woolcoxm/MyHarness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">MyHarness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-05\">2026-10-05</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/woolcoxm/MyHarness/tree/801082b90e182badb93ad3aca1946e4ff9ccfae6\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 801082b90e182badb93ad3aca1946e4ff9ccfae6\">801082b &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously perform coding work through model-selected repository/tool actions and iterative feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unfamiliar codebases, file state, command/build/test failures, tool/provider errors, incomplete requirements, context pressure and long-running goal completion uncertainty. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next inspection/edit/command/subagent action, revise work from returned evidence, and decide when to claim task completion. - Decision owner: the active model-backed MyHarness coding agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Agent::runturn, coding tools, steering, verify hooks, autonomous continuation, permissions/sandboxing, sessions, MCP/LSP bridges, skills and subagent tool. - Closure path: task/context → model chooses action/tool → concrete result or subagent report returns → model revises subsequent work → repeat until completion, interruption or bounded stop. - Boundary reachability: TUI, headless and autonomous entry points instantiate the same first-party agent/tool loop directly. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: without the model actor, the runtime retains tools, validators and persistence but loses the open-ended decisions that transform evidence into coding actions. - Evidence: README; src/agent/mod.rs; src/main.rs; src/tools/mod.rs; src/tools/task.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: external providers supply inference but not first-party organizational ownership.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no distinct first-party inter-S1 coordination loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: multiple subagents can run in parallel and coder subagents may share the workspace, but no shipped mechanism specifically detects and attenuates peer interference among them. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no S2-specific conflict/oscillation decision was found. - Decision owner: not established at S2 level. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: subagent tool restriction, independent contexts, background-task handles, shared cancellation and parent polling. - Closure path: parent delegation/report collection sequences or parallelizes work but does not close a peer-interference attenuation loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: generic delegation and concurrency are not S2 without a concrete disturbance-specific coordination relation. - Evidence: src/tools/task.rs; src/agent/mod.rs; README. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: parallel coder subagents may themselves create workspace contention; the absence of a dedicated coordination path is why this is not credited positively.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no distinct whole-system current-control function is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: turn limits, budgets, background tasks, todo state, permissions and autonomous completion checks regulate one focal coding run and its delegated helpers. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no separate authority holds a whole-system current view and substantively manages a portfolio of current S1 commitments/resources/priorities. - Decision owner: not established at S3 level. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: autonomous budgets, turn caps, todo state, background-task registry, steering, permissions and session state. - Closure path: these mechanisms constrain or inform the same operational loop rather than forming a separate whole-current management loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the parent agent delegates and consumes reports as part of S1 task execution; no distinct metasystemic current-control owner is exposed. - Evidence: src/main.rs; src/agent/mod.rs; src/tools/task.rs; src/tools/todo.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: subagent dispatch alone is insufficient for S3 without a whole-system current view and substantive current-control scope.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge implementation/correctness claims by direct fresh-context review or testing and return findings to the producing parent agent. - Disturbance / variety regulated: defects, incorrect assumptions, security problems and unverified completion claims that may survive the producer&#39;s own reasoning or self-check. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the reviewer/tester subagent decides which directly observed repository/test evidence constitutes a material finding or failure and reports that judgment to the parent. - Decision owner: the separate model-backed subagent instantiated through the first-party task tool. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: fresh AgentState, isolated model context, role-specific tool subsets, documented reviewer/tester task modes, direct workspace/search/read/bash access and final-report return. - Closure path: parent/producer changes or proposes workspace state → parent dispatches reviewer/tester subagent → child directly inspects repository and/or executes verification → child report returns as tool output → parent changes later coding/repair action based on findings. - Boundary reachability: task is a shipped first-party tool and explicitly documents code-review/testing uses; no external reviewer product is required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the child model leaves role/tool plumbing but removes the independent semantic audit judgment over inspected evidence. - Evidence: README; src/tools/task.rs; src/agent/mod.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: dispatch is chosen by the parent and review is not mandatory for every task; the positive state concerns the supported first-party complementary-audit mode.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no externally and prospectively oriented adaptation loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: zero-mem retrieves prior-session evidence, skills provide reusable instructions, web/MCP/LSP tools expose external information and session replay restores past state. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party actor generates and installs future-oriented harness capability adaptations from environmental change. - Decision owner: not established at S4 level. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: zero-mem BM25/entity retrieval, static skills, session replay, web tools, MCP/LSP integrations and context compaction. - Closure path: remembered/external evidence feeds current task execution; it does not close an outside-and-then adaptation cycle back into durable harness capability. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: persistence, retrieval and external research are insufficient for S4 without prospective distinction, adaptation-option generation and capability return. - Evidence: README; src/zeromem.rs; src/skills.rs; src/session.rs; src/mcp.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: users can manually create skills or reconfigure providers, but generic extensibility is not autonomous organizational adaptation.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no identity- or ultimate-policy-level closure is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: permission modes, deny/allow rules, approval prompts, sandbox boundaries, hooks, workspace scoping and authentication constrain ordinary operation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no identity/ultimate-policy issue is routed to a legitimate ultimate authority and returned as a durable system-level policy decision. - Decision owner: not established at S5 level. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: permission engine, plan/ask/auto-edit/yolo modes, sandboxing, hooks, auth, workspace/write guards and configured tool rules. - Closure path: operator configuration and approvals affect current actions but do not close identity-level policy questions for the harness. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: static/configurable safety and access constraints are enforcement mechanisms, not S5. - Evidence: README; src/perms.rs; src/sandbox.rs; src/hooks.rs; src/auth.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: maintainers/operators retain authority outside a run, but development/deployment governance is adjacent to the assessed operating boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/cdfb1b9d4f2e1d13019b2fc9aaa584823469e59e/assessments/myharness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit cdfb1b9d4f2e1d13019b2fc9aaa584823469e59e\">cdfb1b9 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-03T03:50:08+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/amuluze/axiom-agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Axiom</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-03\">2026-10-03</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/amuluze/axiom-agent/tree/da7b98b739a824713d230b2635edb56868553696\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision da7b98b739a824713d230b2635edb56868553696\">da7b98b &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform environment-facing engineering work by interpreting the objective, inspecting artifacts, selecting coding/design/deployment tools, proposing or applying authorized changes, observing results and iterating toward completion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous project structure and code, incomplete task information, tool/provider failures, approval outcomes, changing workspace state, tests/commands, design/browser/remote evidence, context pressure and restart/recovery conditions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next task-specific engineering action/tool, which evidence to gather or state to change, and when the requested operation is complete. - Decision owner: the main model-backed Axiom agent operating through the first-party model/tool runtime. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tool registry/discovery; workspace authorization and approval leases; sandbox and redaction; durable session/recovery journal; context compaction; queues; runtime budgets; provider transport; project docs/skills; checkpoints and branch/retry support. - Closure path: user objective/current session context → model selects action/tool → Axiom enforcement and tool execution against the environment → result/error persisted and returned → next model turn revises action or completes. - Boundary reachability: the normal production desktop runtime directly instantiates the first-party AgentSession/AgentHarness with the product tool runtime; no downstream application author must compose a separate agent loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: if the main model actor is removed while deterministic tools, approvals, persistence and recovery remain, the open-ended engineering choice of what to inspect/change/do next disappears. - Evidence: README.md; runAgentLoop.ts; AgentSession.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: side-effecting tools can require user approval, but approval gates the main agent&#39;s proposed action rather than supplying the engineering judgment itself.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material first-party inter-S1 coordination function is established at the assessed recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: no concrete collision, interference or oscillation among distinct production S1 units is present in the standard organization. Reviewer/explore children are scoped read-only subordinate roles invoked synchronously for evidence/audit rather than peer workers mutating a shared operational domain. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established at S2 scope. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: child scope isolation; read-only child tool set; parent/child budgets; sequential child tool execution; message queues; durable mutation/session journals; workspace authorization. - Closure path: not applicable; no distinct production-S1 interference → attenuation → changed later S1 behavior relation was found. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: SubAgent plurality does not itself establish S2. The frozen runtime supplies reviewer/explorer support units, but no shared-write peer population or concrete inter-operational conflict requiring coordination. - Evidence: SubAgentRuntime.ts; contracts.ts; createToolRegistry.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: separate child model sessions are real actors, but their read-only reviewer/explorer roles do not turn them into mutually interfering production S1s.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no separate whole-system current-control function over a population of operational commitments/resources is established at the assessed recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: run budgets, queue state, approvals, runtime updates, recovery, child delegation limits and lifecycle hooks regulate one main engineering session and its support/audit calls rather than a whole organization of production S1 units. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established at S3 scope. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AgentSession/AgentHarness lifecycle; message queues; mutation journal; turn save points; runtime hooks; subagent parent-run ledger; abort/deadline controls; approval coordinator; recovery/checkpoints. - Closure path: these paths constrain, recover or support the focal operating session. No actor with a whole-system view chooses or revises shared production commitments, priorities, resource allocations or cross-S1 interventions. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the main agent can choose to delegate a reviewer/explorer, but managing support calls inside its own task is ordinary S1 work, not evidence of S3 over distinct operational units. - Evidence: AgentSession.ts; AgentHarness.ts; contracts.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: extensive runtime control and recovery should not be promoted to S3 merely because they supervise execution.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently review specifications/plans/code changes through a separate read-only model session, judge them against direct project evidence and return pass/fail findings into the parent agent&#39;s corrective workflow. - Disturbance / variety regulated: specification/plan defects, implementation bugs, security regressions, inadequate tests, contract violations and cases where the implementer&#39;s ordinary self-assessment would otherwise allow incorrect work to finish. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the child reviewer independently decides a structured pass/fail audit verdict and prioritizes concrete path:line findings from its own scoped read evidence; the parent model receives that verdict as a tool result and owns the subsequent correction/re-review response. - Decision owner: a separate model-backed Axiom reviewer child for the substantive audit judgment. Deterministic verdict parsing and notices only expose/enforce the child&#39;s conclusion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: default subagent:review capability; discoveragenttools; review/inspect/examine tool contracts; separate SubAgent child session; scoped read-only environment; reviewer-specific prompt; optional actual diff supplied by the parent; child/parent budgets; verdict parser and fail notice. - Closure path: main agent implements/specifies/plans → autonomously discovers and invokes reviewer → separate child reads scoped operational artifacts (and code diff when supplied) → child emits pass/fail + findings → first-party tool parses and returns verdict to parent → fail notice and canonical SDD rules direct parent back to repair → parent can change the work and re-run review before finish. - Boundary reachability: production desktop policy grants subagent:review by default, product startup creates and injects the first-party SubAgentRuntime, the review tools are registered in the ordinary tool table, and always-active discoveragenttools lets the main model activate them without developer composition or user approval. - Claim being audited: that the current Task Spec/Plan/implementation is sufficiently correct, safe, tested and contract-compatible to advance or finish. - Ordinary reporting path: the main engineering agent&#39;s own plan/implementation and ordinary tool/test results inside its working session. - Complementary access path: a separately instantiated child model session with a reviewer-specific system prompt and scoped read-only access re-reads the current workspace independently; for code review it can additionally receive the parent-collected Git diff as authoritative change evidence. - Independence boundary: the reviewer has separate child session identity/context, cannot mutate the workspace or inherit the parent&#39;s conversation as an ordinary continuation, and forms its own pass/fail judgment from direct read evidence. It shares the configured model/transport family unless the operator changes that configuration, but the organizational judgment path is a distinct model invocation/session rather than the implementer&#39;s in-band self-report. - Who acts on findings: the main Axiom agent receives the reviewer result. On fail, the tool result explicitly instructs it to return to the previous stage, fix findings and re-delegate review; the standard SDD system prompt independently encodes fail → repair → re-review before finish. A user can explicitly accept risk and override the soft gate. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the reviewer model while retaining the deterministic verdict parser, budgets and fail-message machinery removes the substantive independent defect judgment. The parser cannot manufacture a pass/fail audit finding on its own. - Evidence: runtimePolicy.ts; agentStore.ts; SubAgentRuntime.ts; createReviewerSubAgentTool.ts; buildReviewPrompt.ts; systemPromptSections.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the corrective gate is model-visible and workflow-enforced rather than a deterministic hard block; users can explicitly accept risk. That does not remove the autonomous complementary audit judgment or its normal corrective return path.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material external-and-prospective organizational adaptation loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: project docs/skills, provider/runtime settings, context compaction, session recovery and tool discovery alter current execution context/capability exposure but do not sense future environmental change and autonomously choose a persistent capability/strategy adaptation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established at S4 scope. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: project document scanning/reload; project skill inventory/load; runtime dependency refresh; provider/model settings; context manager; durable session state and recovery. - Closure path: changed project files/configuration can be rescanned into later current prompts, but no first-party prospective scan → adaptation option generation → selected persistent organizational capability change → operational return loop is present. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: generic ability to write project files plus later reload does not establish a function-specific adaptation decision. The reload machinery consumes externally/currently changed artifacts rather than deciding a future capability change itself. - Evidence: projectDocs.ts; loadProjectSkills.ts; projectDocsRefresh.ts; AgentSession.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: durable reloadable project artifacts can influence later behavior, but persistence/reload alone is not S4 without a prospective adaptation function and decisive adaptation right.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material runtime identity or ultimate-policy closure is established at the assessed recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: workspace authorization, approval modes, capability policy, prompts, project docs and user decisions constrain work, but they do not form a first-party identity/ultimate-policy adjudication loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established at S5 scope. - Decision owner: not established within the Axiom runtime. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: production runtime policy; workspace authorization; approval coordinator/leases; system prompt; user/project documentation; settings; sandbox/network/security enforcement. - Closure path: policy/configuration is supplied or changed externally and enforced/loaded into operation. No identity/ultimate-policy issue is routed to a legitimate first-party S5 authority, resolved there and returned as an authoritative organization-wide policy change. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the main/reviewer agents operate under the supplied capability/security/project boundaries but do not possess authority to redefine Axiom&#39;s purpose, identity or ultimate policy. - Evidence: runtimePolicy.ts; ApprovalCoordinator.ts; systemPromptSections.ts; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: user approval can override a soft review gate and project owners can author durable guidance. Generic approval/configuration is not promoted to S5 without an identity/ultimate-policy function.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/70175cff60657032ff976a1b5802131728209974/assessments/axiom.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 70175cff60657032ff976a1b5802131728209974\">70175cf &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-10T20:02:22+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Maurdekye/orgtree\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Orgtree</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-10\">2026-10-10</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Maurdekye/orgtree/tree/0008ccb94edfb986396c49ced3e2d2c885e1fb16\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 0008ccb94edfb986396c49ced3e2d2c885e1fb16\">0008ccb &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: execute bounded coding/investigation tasks through separately instantiated provider-backed autonomous working agents, supported by Orgtree persistent organizations and tooling. - Disturbance / variety regulated: evolving user tasks, codebase complexity, failing tests, external model/tool feedback and revised instructions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: each hired model-driven agent chooses steps and code/tool activity within its charter, provider and granted scope. - Decision owner: selected Claude/Codex/OpenRouter-model agent actor hosted by Orgtree; private provider cognition is an external implementation, not Orgtree-authored organizational logic. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: persistent agent nodes, charters, provider adapters and session runners, tool/folder grants, CLI/MCP tools and queued conversation. - Closure path: user/coordinator supplies job and charter → model-driven agent starts under configured provider → model makes work/tool decisions against repo/task feedback → changes/response reach retained transcript and docket → next local agent step reflects updated context. - Boundary reachability: the Windows packaged engine + desktop explicitly creates agents, starts their model turns and offers first-party OpenRouter/provider adapters; not a development-only agent. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: without the model-driven actor Orgtree has no comparable discretionary task-solving capability; retaining a chart and provider launch without inference only transports work. - Evidence: README.md; supervisor.py; openrouterharness.py; openrouter.py; providers.py; mcptool.py - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high for assembled installed provider mode. - Caveats: OpenRouter model inference and external coding-agent internal tool execution are third-party; Orgtree&#39;s own persistent control wiring is the credited harness boundary.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate conflicting resource ownership by separate operating agents attempting concurrent work or integration. - Disturbance / variety regulated: two active workers may try to land incompatible changes or mutate the same shared resource/candidate/base under separate responsibilities. - Decisive decision or feedback right: grant one held reservation and refuse a competing claimant while live owner/heartbeat exists, with scoped recovery only after staleness proof. - Decision owner: deterministic first-party resource reservation handler; workers/coordinator select target resources but the conflict gate is constructor-enforced, not autonomously resolved. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: persistent reservation rows under document lock, resource key, candidate/base pairing, owner identity, lease/heartbeat, error projection of holder, renewal/release/land. - Closure path: agent A acquires scoped shared resource → agent B requests same resource with incompatible ownership → first-party ReservationError names still-live holder and refuses B → B must defer/reconcile work, preventing duplicate land → release/recovery makes resource available for subsequent work. - Boundary reachability: reservation module is in shipped Python engine&#39;s org document/API control path and is described as runtime durable scoped resource reservations, with standard agent-facing coordination tools. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the decisive arbitration is deterministic; there is no independent agent choosing winners. This is S2=C for a specific inter-worker collision, not generic organization-chart messaging. - Distinct S1 units: two independently hired provider-backed coding-agent nodes working toward commits against one shared repository/integration resource. - Inter-S1 disturbance: simultaneous integrations or mutable resource claims create conflicting ownership and duplicate/stale actions. - Attenuating coordination relation: reservations.execute(action=acquire) checks active holder, normalized resource and scope, denies different live claimant unless holder actually ceased; lease alone never steals live claim. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: refusal returns holder/resource and reason to calling agent, so it defers or selects other work; only a valid held actor can perform related mutation/landing. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: a concrete inter-S1 exclusive-resource conflict is damped at admission rather than relying on ordinary mail, hierarchical titles, queue ordering, or shared storage. - Evidence: reservations.py; api.py; ledger.py; mcptool.py; README.md - Basis: structural + explicit. - Confidence: medium-high for scoped reservation mode. - Caveats: reservation metadata does not itself sandbox files, and overlap query is advisory for declared paths; credit is restricted to exclusive resource ownership arbitration.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: whole-current organization control by a designated persistent coordinator agent managing job assignment, staffing, review gates and blocked work across subordinate agent units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: duplicate staffing, missing/blocked assignments, forgotten post-compaction decisions and unattended review/implementation tasks across active workers. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the configured coordinator model selects whether to create/assign/reassign docket tasks, hire staff with work, inspect subordinate evidence, or escalate disputed facts to user. - Decision owner: an explicitly hired model-backed coordinator in the supported first-party preset/agent-management mode; neither static org hierarchy nor credit checks autonomously owns these choices. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: bundled coordinator charter, first-party orgtreestaff, orgtreework, hire/rehire, task ownership notifications, direct chat/mail, active org/docket state and transcript view. - Closure path: coordinator sees user request and persistent docket/agent/tree state → interprets workload/ownership/report evidence → decides which unit should perform/review work → first-party staffing/assignment API writes owner and delivers wake instruction → worker&#39;s next agent turn executes and returns progress/evidence. - Boundary reachability: README explicitly supports a human hiring a coordinator and bundled charter presets; live agent-facing orgtreestaff tool performs atomic seat+work assignment, and associated hire/work operations are in the packaged engine. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the coordinator model exercises discretion over present organizational allocations; removing it leaves only human input and deterministic staffing APIs, not equivalent automated current control. - Whole-system current view: coordinator can inspect the parent org tree and its own subordinates, shared docket, messages, transcripts and status/evidence; the charter directs it to check for already running work before staffing. - Current-control decision scope: decide who owns each new feature/investigation, when a review is required, who responds to a blocked specialist, and when to retain/retire agent seats. - Evidence: README.md; coordinator.md; mcptool.py; api.py; ledger.py; supervisor.py - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high in supported coordinator operating mode. - Caveats: the coordinator is optional and must be hired/chartered; a passive org chart and basic hierarchy alone do not yield S3=A, and no credit is given to a private provider team manager.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: ? - Function: independent challenge of ordinary operational/implementation reports with direct evidence and corrective feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: implementation claims that misstate source changes, tests or production behavior. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the review-workflow charter proposes distinct redteam reviewer roles and coordinator approval, but a guaranteed separately instantiated independent audit-and-return closure is not shown for all supported organizations. - Decision owner: undetermined for first-party enacted S3; optional model reviewer would own judgment only if explicitly hired, given direct evidence, and returned findings alter later work. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: review charter preset, code/test evidence, docket review items, transcript/file inspection and work-evidence git provenance. - Closure path: a reviewer may inspect implementation and report findings; actual invocation, independence of reviewer from implementer and mandatory correction/acceptance loop remain mode-dependent and were not reconstructed to publication threshold. - Boundary reachability: review charter and tooling ship, but user-selected workflow alone does not prove operational closure in current assessment boundary. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a role label or proposed review checklist cannot substitute for an evidenced independent, different decision owner and a corrective return. - Claim being audited: agent implementing a feature has delivered correct code. - Ordinary reporting path: implementer conversation, docket progress and claimed completion. - Complementary access path: reviewer direct source/commit/test inspection and runtime work-evidence tools are possible. - Independence boundary: role separation is configurable rather than guaranteed; no canonical independent audit workflow bound in all modes. - Who acts on findings: coordinator/user may require changes, but obligatory first-party return from independent findings is not conclusively established. - Evidence: review-workflow.md; coordinator.md; workitems.py; workevidence.py; mcptool.py - Basis: explicit + unresolved. - Confidence: medium in uncertain closure. - Caveats: do not turn the charter&#39;s redteam guidance into positive S3 on documentation alone; retain ?.\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: ? - Function: prospective monitoring of environmental possibilities with chosen adaptation options that modify future organization capabilities. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changes in provider models, incoming work and external demands not yet handled by current staffing. - Decisive decision or feedback right: operator can change providers/accounts/charters; reactive account fallback and persisted history do not show independently owned prospective environment scanning and adaptation choices. - Decision owner: unresolved. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: persistent agent history, provider availability, account fallback, model selection and handoff. - Closure path: switch to another account may allow an interrupted turn to finish, but no evidential prospective exploration → future capability-change decision → return into current operations is established. - Boundary reachability: provider/continuity components ship; positive S4 not inferred from them. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: resilience/fallback is a reactive resource workaround rather than outside-and-then strategic intelligence. - External distinction: external model/provider availability changes are observed reactively. - Future / prospective distinction: no separately evidenced future-option research, beyond current-task orchestration. - Adaptation option generated: unverified. - Path back into current capability / S3: no S4-specific returned adaptation established. - Evidence: providers.py; accounts.py; handoff.py; README.md - Basis: structural + unresolved. - Confidence: medium in insufficient evidence. - Caveats: future configured roles might act prospectively, but no such supported first-party loop was proven at this frozen ref.\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: ? - Function: decide ultimate purpose and identity of the whole organization when constitutive conflicts arise. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changes to legitimate goals, final authority and organizational identity rather than ordinary permission/staffing. - Decisive decision or feedback right: human operator sets charters and capacity/access constraints, but no distinct ultimate-policy issue→authority→returned operation was reconstructed. - Decision owner: human owner configures charter/permissions and deployment boundaries; distinct S5 ownership unresolved. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: persistent agent identities, charter text, hierarchy, folder/tool grants, capacity and delegation budget, audience request path. - Closure path: charters and permissions affect later agent execution, but this is normal configuration rather than evidence of an enacted identity-level decision loop. - Boundary reachability: charter/edit controls are standard shipped mode; no positive S5 based on them. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: an autonomous coordinator following a founder&#39;s charter is not authorized to define or change the organization&#39;s ultimate identity by being called &#39;coordinator&#39;. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: not established as a resolved runtime issue. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: no positive S5 parent or autonomous mode demonstrated. - Return-to-operation path: config reaches agent prompts and enforcement but does not prove whole-purpose adjudication. - Evidence: README.md; schema.py; mcptool.py; api.py; coordinator.md - Basis: structural + unresolved. - Confidence: medium in insufficient evidence. - Caveats: persisted roles and delegated grants are not organizational ultimate identity decisions.\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/5f4e494b65b62449b1c3199d40892c08bb78c78b/assessments/orgtree.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 5f4e494b65b62449b1c3199d40892c08bb78c78b\">5f4e494 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-24T17:03:31+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Vinay-veeragani/Multi-Agent-Orchestration\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Multi-Agent Orchestration Engine</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-24\">2026-09-24</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Vinay-veeragani/Multi-Agent-Orchestration/tree/e6c34462af045d7e53d383103346362351c96353\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision e6c34462af045d7e53d383103346362351c96353\">e6c3446 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform assigned operational task work through autonomous agent reason/act loops that can choose tool use and produce task outputs. - Disturbance / variety regulated: task uncertainty, model/tool feedback, external data returned by allowed tools and local decisions required to satisfy each delegated instruction. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose task-local reasoning steps, permitted tool calls and when to return the assigned result. - Decision owner: the autonomous model actor inside each first-party AgentRuntime invocation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AgentDefinition, model routing, tool schemas, policy authoriser, budget checks, iteration limits, tool execution and output validation. - Closure path: supervisor/workflow assigns a node instruction → AgentRuntime builds the agent context → autonomous model reasons and requests permitted tools → tool results feed back into the same loop → the agent returns a validated AgentOutput into execution state. - Boundary reachability: the shared AgentRuntime is the standard execution path for registered agents, including dynamically delegated nodes; it is not a benchmark-only or development-only actor. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic runtime code limits and transports the loop, but substantive task-local reasoning/tool choices are made by the model actor. - Evidence: src/orchestration/agents/runtime.py; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: provider/model internals are environmental; ownership is credited only to the autonomous role reached through the first-party runtime.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material first-party run-level S2 coordination function is established at the reviewed boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the runtime contains parallel execution, joins, locks, semaphores and dependency scheduling, but the inspected standard paths do not establish a specific inter-S1 interference/conflict/oscillation together with a function-specific coordination decision/feedback path that changes subsequent autonomous agent behavior. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for S2. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: workflow edges and join policies sequence/aggregate work; maxconcurrentnodes limits parallel execution; Redis locks/semaphores protect process-level mutual exclusion and capacity; supervisor delegation controls current work allocation. These are not promoted to S2 merely because they coordinate execution machinery. - Closure path: not applicable for the negative finding. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the reviewed runtime has generic scheduling, synchronization and current-control mechanisms, but no evidenced S2-specific autonomous coordination discretion over a reconstructed inter-S1 disturbance. - Evidence: docs/workflow-engine.md; src/orchestration/coordination/redis.py; docs/dynamic-orchestration.md. - Basis: explicit + structural negative search. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a downstream workflow can use the supplied synchronization primitives to construct stronger coordination behavior; general framework expressiveness is not sufficient for S2=C.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: regulate the current execution as a whole by observing live run state and deciding which agents/work should run next, when failed work should retry or be replanned, when parallel work should be introduced, and when the run should finalize or fail. - Disturbance / variety regulated: incomplete or failed work, changing execution progress, unsuitable delegation, exhausted/invalid routes, current resource/budget constraints, blocked work and uncertainty about whether more operational work is required. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose a RoutingDecision over delegate, paralleldelegate, retry, replan, requesthumanapproval, responddirectly, finalize or fail against the current ExecutionState. - Decision owner: the autonomous supervisor model actor in the standard dynamic-orchestration mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: typed routing schema, semantic validation, agent registry, deterministic fallback, ExecutionOrchestrator, workflow compilation/execution, budget meter, policy engine, checkpoint/event persistence and cancel token. - Closure path: current execution/node state is supplied to Supervisor.decide() → autonomous supervisor selects a current-control action → first-party schema/semantic validators accept or degrade it → ExecutionOrchestrator compiles/applies the valid action → agents/nodes execute under that decision → resulting node state/output returns to the next supervisor turn. - Boundary reachability: ExecutionOrchestrator explicitly calls the first-party Supervisor each turn in the supported dynamic execution path; this is the ordinary runtime design rather than an evaluator-only layer. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the supervisor model owns the discretionary current-control choice when it produces a valid decision. Deterministic validators, schedulers, budgets and policy gates enforce feasibility/constraints without inheriting the decision right. The heuristic fallback is a degraded deterministic mode rather than the basis for A. - Evidence: src/orchestration/supervisor/supervisor.py; docs/supervisor-and-routing.md; docs/dynamic-orchestration.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: static user-authored workflows and deterministic heuristic fallback are separate supported control paths; the A finding is specifically grounded in the standard autonomous dynamic-supervisor mode. Action-scoped human approval does not by itself establish an S3 parent mode. - Whole-system current view: the supervisor prompt is built from current ExecutionState, registered agent summaries, tool surface and optional budget/workflow state; it can validate retries/replans against actual node status and current graph state. - Current-control decision scope: delegation/allocation, parallel fan-out, retry, graph replan, approval escalation, direct response, finalization and failure of the current execution.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no materially distinct complementary audit path over operational S1 reality is established in the reviewed standard distribution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: routing/schema/policy validators can reject invalid decisions or calls, but they inspect conformance of the same control/tool path rather than independently challenge operational claims/results against alternative evidence. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for S3. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Pydantic/schema validation, Supervisor.validatedecision(), policy checks, output validation, checkpoint consistency and benchmark/evaluation code provide correctness/enforcement surfaces but do not become S3 by label or verification vocabulary. - Closure path: not applicable for the negative finding. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the reviewed validators are in-line deterministic gates around ordinary operation/current control; no separate autonomous or constructor audit actor with complementary access and corrective feedback into the same run was found. - Evidence: src/orchestration/supervisor/supervisor.py; docs/supervisor-and-routing.md; src/orchestration/agents/runtime.py. - Basis: explicit + structural negative search. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: repository evaluation/benchmark infrastructure is adjacent first-party evidence and was excluded from runtime ownership under Methodology 0.3.6 boundary rules.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no external-and-prospective organizational adaptation loop is established in the reviewed standard distribution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: retry/replan/model routing respond to the current task/run, and checkpoints preserve current execution state, but none establishes a separate outside/future sensing → adaptation option → returned capability change loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for S4. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: supervisor replan, node retry policies, model routing, durable checkpoints/resume, configuration and benchmark/evaluation infrastructure support current execution/recovery without establishing prospective adaptation. - Closure path: not applicable for the negative finding. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: current-run replanning is S3/current-control behavior; no first-party actor is evidenced as changing persistent organizational capability from external/future distinctions. - Evidence: docs/dynamic-orchestration.md; docs/checkpointing-and-resume.md; docs/evaluation-benchmark.md. - Basis: explicit + structural negative search. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: retries, replanning and benchmark-driven development may improve the project in practice; they are not automatically run-level S4.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no runtime identity/ultimate-policy governance loop is established at the assessed execution boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: configured budgets, tool permissions, risk rules and approval gates constrain operation, but they are pre-existing operational policy/enforcement rather than an authoritative decision over organizational identity or ultimate policy. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for S5. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: deny-by-default tool policy, hard budgets, risk-level approval requirements, operator-supplied configuration and action-scoped human approvals. - Closure path: not applicable for the negative finding. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the runtime enforces configured rules and can pause for action-specific approval, but no first-party standard mode was found in which an autonomous or parent authority adjudicates an identity/ultimate-policy question and returns a newly decided policy into subsequent organization-wide operation. - Evidence: docs/budget-and-policies.md; docs/human-in-the-loop.md; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural negative search. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a human may approve or modify one risky tool action; Methodology 0.3.6 explicitly does not promote generic operational approval to S5 parent governance.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/13ed3855b7ea0d890c83aadea7732422ca8db7f2/assessments/multi-agent-orchestration.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 13ed3855b7ea0d890c83aadea7732422ca8db7f2\">13ed385 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-11T22:54:20+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Omerfaruk-aydn/Atlas-Agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Atlas Agent</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-11\">2026-10-11</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Omerfaruk-aydn/Atlas-Agent/tree/3d0d63e20d9d2221be9a6e6ab4fc0350df5237f5\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 3d0d63e20d9d2221be9a6e6ab4fc0350df5237f5\">3d0d63e &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Model-directed coding operations in the first-party Go session runtime; includes shell/filesystem/source tools, provider/tool feedback and independently model-driven bounded subagent instances. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing user instructions, source/code errors, build/environment changes, provider responses and tool results. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Main SessionAgent model selects work and tools after observing files, commands and previous responses; bounded subagent model chooses its own scoped actions. - Decision owner: First-party sessionAgent model loop, or generated model-driven child on delegated work. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Coordinator model/provider builder, registered toolset, session persistence, cancel and message queue, prompt hooks, budgets and permission gates. - Closure path: User goal → model Stream/tool selection → tool execution/result → next model step or reply, with persistent messages and bounded recovery. - Boundary reachability: Shipped Atlas executable uses Coordinator.Run with buildAgent; it constructs SessionAgent and first-party worktree tools. The user need not bring an external coding-agent CLI as the actor. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Without first-party model-selected actions the fixed tool registry and turn controls do not decide which code fix satisfies the user. - Evidence: internal/agent/coordinator.go, internal/agent/coordinator.go, internal/agent/agent.go, internal/agent/agent.go - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: Prevent provider-wide rate-limit interference among parallel model-driven child S1 sessions launched by one task organization. - Distinct S1 units: Multiple independently executing SessionAgent children with individually selected named task prompts created by the agent/delegate/orchestrate/debate tool paths. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Simultaneous provider calls from a large fan-out cause shared service throttling (429 bursts), degrading each child&#39;s progress; source explicitly calls out this concrete failure mode. - Attenuating coordination relation: Configured MaxConcurrentSubAgents creates a channel-semaphore admission gate shared across child runs within the respective delegation tool; waiting children pause instead of starting simultaneous provider use. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: A child acquires a slot or cancels while queued; after a previous child releases its slot, admitted child performs its model/tool loop; results are returned to the caller to inform subsequent task decisions. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: This is not merely selecting an agent or forwarding messages: concurrent independently model-driven S1 work would hit a concrete shared-provider overload disturbance; the constructor dynamically gates and releases admission before subsequent child execution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Inter-child request concurrency and provider 429 overload within a configured rate-limited environment. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Admit/wait/cancel child execution based on a concurrency slot, then release the slot after completion; the upper model selects subtask goals but does not adjudicate slot policy. - Decision owner: Deterministic first-party concurrencyLimiter constructor (C) rather than an autonomous S2 decision model. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Go channel semaphore, tool-level limiter, cancellation token, WaitGroup/join and result multiplexing. - Closure path: Concurrent child requests → slot classification/admission or wait → provider calls serialized to capacity → release on finish → queued child proceeds, returns result to parent. - Boundary reachability: agent, orchestrate, delegate, and debate tool builders register limiter when maxconcurrentsubagents is set above zero; this is packaged Atlas code, not an external team orchestrator. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Agent decides decomposition, but deterministic runtime alone enforces the response to rate-limit contention. - Evidence: internal/agent/subagentlimit.go, internal/agent/orchestratetool.go, internal/agent/orchestratetool.go, internal/agent/delegatetool.go, internal/agent/agenttool.go, internal/config/config.go - Basis: explicit / structural - Confidence: high for constructor, medium for measured effect - Caveats: An unconfigured (zero) cap is unlimited; positive C is explicitly bounded to deployments setting a finite cap. It does not prevent semantic write conflicts between children.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No separately owned organization-wide discretionary supervisor responsible for continuously reallocating priorities/resources across operational S1 teams. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Session cost, tool permissions, user goals, concurrent prompts, retries and current task health. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The S1 root can plan its work and delegate; Goal mode may automatically start the next turn, while fixed budget and session policy enforce caps; neither supplies a distinct whole-current S3 owner. - Decision owner: Current coding agent for task choices and Go runtime/operator for task caps, no independent supervisory S3 decision right. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: goalRun budget/verification, per-session dispatch mutex, cost guards, orchestration tool, per-subagent S2 semaphore, advisor diagnostic. - Closure path: Current goal turns and local enforcement update the same task stream; no distinct whole-current negotiated resource commitment or cross-unit managerial intervention is returned. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Goal continuation and current task planning are within S1; provider concurrency protection is S2, while independent turn review is S3, not by aggregation S3. - Evidence: internal/agent/goal.go, internal/agent/coordinator.go, internal/agent/agent.go, internal/agent/orchestratetool.go - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Model-owned separate read-only second opinion on a completed producing agent turn with concrete code inspection and corrective advice to next turn. - Claim being audited: Producer&#39;s final coding response satisfies user request and any source-code outcome it asserts is substantiated. - Ordinary reporting path: Main coding agent&#39;s user-facing final answer and its ordinary tool results; this path may be mistaken or overly confident. - Complementary access path: Optional configured advisor role builds a separate model invocation with only glob, grep, ls, view read-only tools; it can inspect actual working tree instead of relying solely on the producer summary. - Independence boundary: Advisor is a distinct model-driven checking pass, and optional escalate role can independently inspect code for flagged concerns. It shares the same first-party runtime and configured provider ecosystem, so not an externally or organizationally independent reviewer. - Who acts on findings: Advisor injects NIT/CONCERN/BLOCKER note through advisorNotes into the next main prompt; concern/blocker can also notify the user. The next producing Agent decides corrective action, not a hard mandatory merge gate. - Disturbance / variety regulated: False completion claims and unintended code changes missed by the production turn. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Second model chooses whether, which files, and how to inspect; judges severity and writes concrete issue for the next coding turn. - Decision owner: First-party model-driven Advisor, so A where advisor is enabled and role resolved; escalation may use another model without changing main owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Role-specific read-only tool list, result truncation, cadence EveryNTurns, timeout, severity parser, session note queue and optional user notification. - Closure path: Completed turn → advisor raw-source inspection/judgment → stored concern → injectAdvisorNote into next root turn → subsequent corrective work or user-visible warning. The pass is advisory/fail-open, so no forced correction guarantee. - Boundary reachability: coordinator.buildAgent constructs advisor only in ordinary non-subagent session when options.advisor.enabled and a model role are configured. sessionAgent.Run launches it asynchronously after turn; next Run injects note. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The audit judgment and decision to invoke raw code tools depend on the advisor model, unlike a static test or heuristic. The runtime only schedules and transports its finding. - Evidence: internal/agent/coordinator.go, internal/agent/coordinator.go, internal/agent/advisor.go, internal/agent/agent.go, internal/agent/agent.go - Basis: explicit / structural - Confidence: high for reachable conditional path; medium for actual audit efficacy - Caveats: Optional mode may be disabled by default, timed-out, skipped by cadence, or conclude NONE. Advisor report is not mandatory correction enforcement, and the goal judge&#39;s transcript-only confirmation is not treated as independent raw-source auditing.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No bounded source proof of a future-external environment sensing, strategic option selection and accepted change to organization-wide future capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Provider failures, historical prompts, memory and current goal continuation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Goal mode pursues a current user objective; model fallback and memory change execution continuity, not a prospective autonomous future operating policy. - Decision owner: User/configuration and ordinary task agent for model choices, no first-party prospective adaptation body. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Memory/factstore, context summary, fallback models, goal progress, templates and docs for future features. - Closure path: Existing context and provider choices may be applied inside current tasks, but no externally sourced prospective capability option is adopted into governance/operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Persistence, task resume, failover and alternate models do not imply an S4 intelligence loop. - Evidence: internal/agent/goal.go, internal/agent/memorystore.go, internal/agent/modelfallback.go, internal/agent/coordinator.go - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No autonomous or first-party parent-governed ultimate-policy/identity issue-deliberation with legitimate authoritative return at this agent recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unsafe shell use, cost overrun, tool permissions and user preferences. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Developer/operator configures permissions/model roles; runtime gates apply them locally; user approves or rejects current actions. - Decision owner: External user and fixed local policy enforcement, not an S5 governance organ with issue and binding policy decision. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Permission approvals, budget/session caps, tool access profiles, prompt templates, CLI configuration. - Closure path: Individual tools are authorized or denied; there is no identity-level dispute → legitimate authority → binding revised policy return. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Configured safe execution is not self-governing identity-policy choice. - Evidence: internal/agent/coordinator.go, internal/agent/agent.go, internal/config/config.go - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/f37ef211c66e5b5748caf83479e9ffce387f19dc/assessments/atlas-agent.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit f37ef211c66e5b5748caf83479e9ffce387f19dc\">f37ef21 &#8599;</a></td></tr>"];
